hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3631","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from Malih ibn Abdullah al-Sa'di, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The one who raises his head before the imam and lowers it before him, his forelock is in the hand of Satan."
"3632","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi narrated to us, he said: Al-Bara ibn Azib, and he is not a liar, narrated to us that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised and said: Allah hears the one who praises Him, not one man among us would bend his back until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell in prostration, then we would fall in prostration."
"3633","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Nafi ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have grown heavy, so do not hasten ahead of me in standing, and do not hasten ahead in prostration."
"3634","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: One does not bow before the imam and does not rise before him."
"3635","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Suhaym ibn Nawfal, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: Do not hasten ahead of your imams in bowing or prostration, and if one of you gets ahead, let him remain to the extent of what he got ahead by."
"3636","From Abd al-Wahhab, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Ya'qub ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from al-Harith ibn Makhlad, from his father, he said: Umar said: Any man who raises his head before the imam in bowing or prostration should keep his head down for as long as he raised it."
"3637","From a man, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Badr narrate, from Ali ibn Shayban, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever raises his head from bowing before the imam has no prayer."
"3638","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was once leading his Companions in prayer, when a blind man came and fell into a waterhole containing water, and some of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said: Whoever laughed should repeat his ablution, then repeat his prayer."
"3639","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aliyah al-Riyahi, that a blind man fell into a well while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was leading his Companions in prayer, and some of those praying with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded: Whoever among you laughed should repeat the prayer. Ma'mar said: And Ayyub informed me, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah, the like of that."
"3640","From al-Thawri, from Khalid, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was leading the people in prayer, a man with bad eyesight came and fell into a well near the mosque. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded: Whoever laughed should repeat the ablution and repeat the prayer."
"3641","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: When a man laughs in prayer, he restarts the ablution and restarts the prayer."
"3642","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about that, he said: There is no ablution for laughing."
"3643","From Ma'mar, from Matar al-Warraq, from Shu'ayb, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: When a man laughs in prayer, he repeats the prayer but does not repeat the ablution. From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, the like of it."
"3644","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that he saw a man laughing and ordered him to repeat the prayer."
"3645","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that he ordered his companions because of laughing to repeat the prayer, and not to repeat the ablution."
"3646","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you laugh deliberately in prayer, then guffaw, you have broken your prayer. I said: What if I laughed forgetfully in two prostrations while thinking I had finished? He said: I do not know; perhaps if you complete what remains upon what has passed, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, that would suffice you; rather it is his saying: it is made up for you."
"3647","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Smiling does not break the prayer. He said: I said: Do I prostrate with it the two prostrations of forgetfulness? If you guffaw and you have an odd (witr) prayer, then add a rak'ah to make it even, then begin your prayer anew."
"3648","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you guffaw along with the imam, you have broken your prayer, so begin your prayer then along with him."
"3649","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Smiling does not break the prayer. He said: I said: Do I prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: If you wish, and it is more beloved to me that you do."
"3650","Al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Smiling does not break the prayer, but guffawing breaks it."
"3651","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Smiling does not break the prayer."
"3652","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Smiling does not break the prayer until one bursts out laughing or guffaws."
"3653","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you smiled and your teeth showed, that does not break your prayer."
"3654","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, he said: If one grins it does not harm him until he guffaws. I said to him: What is grinning? He said: His teeth showing."
"3655","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Asim ibn Ubaydullah ibn Asim informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah informed me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be rulers after me who pray the prayer at its time and delay it past its time, so pray it with them; if they pray it at its time and you pray it with them, it is for you and for them, and if they delay it past its time and you pray it with them, it is for you and against them. Whoever leaves the community dies a death of ignorance, and whoever breaks the covenant and dies breaking his covenant will come on the Day of Resurrection with no proof for him."
"3656","Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: I asked Abdullah ibn al-Samit, and he is the nephew of Abu Dharr, about the rulers when they delay the prayer, so he struck my knee and said: I asked Abu Dharr about that, and he did to me as I did to you and struck my knee, and narrated to me that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did to him as he did to me and struck his knee as he struck my knee, and said: Pray the prayer at its time. He said: If you catch it with them, then pray, and let none of you say: I have already prayed, so I will not pray."
"3657","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad delayed the prayer, so I asked Abdullah ibn Samit, and he struck my thigh, then said: I asked my friend Abu Dharr, and he struck my thigh, then said: I asked my friend, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he struck my thigh, and said: Pray the prayer at its time; if you catch it then pray with them, and let none of you say: I have already prayed, so I will not pray."
"3658","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu al-Muthanna, from the son of the wife of Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, he said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: There will come rulers whom things will occupy so that they do not pray the prayer at its appointed time. A man said: O Messenger of Allah, should I then pray with them? He said: Yes."
"3659","Ma'mar reported to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn Samit, from Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be rulers who pray the prayer at its time and delay it past its time, so pray the prayer at its time, and if they delay it, you have secured your prayer."
"3660","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Dharr: How will it be with you when they expel you from Madinah? He said: I will go to the Holy Land. He said: How will it be with you when they expel you from it? He said: I will come to Madinah. He said: How will it be with you when they expel you from it? He said: I will take my sword and strike with it. He said: No, but listen and obey, even if he is a black slave. He said: When Abu Dharr went out to al-Rabadhah, he found there a black slave boy of Uthman, who gave the call and the iqamah, then said: Come forward, O Abu Dharr. He said: No, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to listen and obey, even if he is a black slave. He said: So he came forward and he prayed behind him."
"3661","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Suhayb and Abu al-Muthanna, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be rulers over you who delay the prayer; pray the prayer at its time, and when you catch it then pray, and make your prayer with them voluntary."
"3662","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Mas'ud said to his companions: I do not hold you back from the time, and he led them in Zuhr, I think he said: when the sun passed its zenith, then said: There will be rulers over you who delay the prayer, so pray the prayer at its time, and if you catch it with them then pray."
"3663","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: You are in a time of few preachers and many scholars; they lengthen the prayer and shorten the sermon, and there will come upon you a time of many preachers and few scholars; they lengthen the sermon and delay the prayer until it is said: This is the rising of the dead. He said: I said to him: What is the rising of the dead? He said: When the sun has become very yellow; whoever reaches that should pray the prayer at its time, and if he is detained then let him pray with them, and make his prayer alone the obligatory one, and make his prayer with them voluntary."
"3664","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Mas'ud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How will it be with you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, when there are rulers over you who extinguish the Sunnah and delay the prayer past its appointed time? He said: How do you command me, O Messenger of Allah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You ask me, son of Umm Abd, what to do! There is no obedience to a creature in disobedience to Allah."
"3665","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from Mahdi, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: How will you be, O Mahdi, when your best are overcome, and your youngest are put in charge over you, and prayer is prayed outside its appointed time? He said: I said: I do not know. He said: Do not be a tax collector, nor a headman, nor a policeman, nor a courier, and pray the prayer at its time."
"3666","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Al-Walid ibn Uqbah once delayed the prayer, so Ibn Mas'ud ordered the caller to announce the prayer, then came forward and led the people in prayer. Al-Walid sent to him: What have you done? Has something new come to you from the Commander of the Believers, or did you innovate? Ibn Mas'ud said: Neither of that happened, but Allah and His Messenger refused for us to wait for you with our prayer while you attend to your need."
"3667","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It reached me that Ibn Mas'ud said: There will arise after you officials who do not pray the prayer at its appointed time, and when they do that, then pray it at its appointed time."
"3668","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: As for what delays the prayer so that one prays it wastefully? He said: Praying the congregation with him is more beloved to me. I said: What is with you! Will you not stop at the saying of Ibn Mas'ud about that? He said: The congregation is more beloved to me as long as it is not missed. I said: Even if the sun has yellowed toward sunset and reached the mountain tops? He said: Yes, as long as it has not set."
"3669","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from al-Nakha'i and Khaythamah, he said: They used to pray Zuhr and Asr with al-Hajjaj, and he would delay into the evening."
"3670","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, he said: Al-Hajjaj gave the sermon on Friday and delayed the prayer, and a man wanted to jump at him, while the people held him back."
"3671","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, and from al-Zuhri, and from Qatadah, that they used to pray with the rulers even if they delayed."
"3672","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Al-Walid once delayed Friday until evening. He said: So I prayed Zuhr before sitting, then prayed Asr while sitting as he preached. He said: I place my hands on my knees and gesture with my head."
"3673","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Abi Isma'il, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Al-Walid once delayed the prayer, and I saw them gesturing while sitting."
"3674","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, that they used to pray Zuhr when Zuhr time came, and when Asr time came they prayed Asr in the mosque in their place, while Ibn Ziyad used to delay Zuhr and Asr."
"3675","From al-Thawri, from Uqbah, from Abu Wa'il, that he used to join in congregation with al-Mukhtar the liar."
"3676","From Isra'il, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq, he said: He used to order us to pray Friday in our homes, then come to the mosque, and that was because al-Hajjaj used to delay the prayer."
"3677","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Prayer is good; I do not care who shares in it with me."
"3678","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Abu Ja'far: That Hasan and Husayn used to hasten when they heard Marwan's caller, while they reviled him, praying with him."
"3679","From Abu al-Ashhab, a shaykh of Basra, he said: I asked Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, while the Khawarij had risen against us, and said: O Abu Nasr, what is your view on praying behind these? He said: The Qur'an is your imam; pray with them as long as they pray it at its time."
"3680","From al-Thawri and others, from al-Awza'i, from Umayr ibn Hani, he said: I saw Ibn Umar, Ibn al-Zubayr, Najdah, and al-Hajjaj, while Ibn Umar would say: They plunge into the Fire as flies plunge into broth; and when he heard the caller he would hasten to him, meaning their caller, and pray with him."
"3681","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: An imam who does not complete the prayer, should I keep away from prayer? He said: Rather pray with him, and complete what you can even if he stands. I said: Likewise if in the desert with the imam and he does not complete? He said: Likewise, so complete it yourself. I said: I was with a man on a journey and we found, and he would lead me and not complete, so should I pray alone? He said: Rather pray with him and complete; two is more beloved to me than one, and three is more beloved to me than two."
"3682","From Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Ibrahim, from al-A'mash, he said: I said to Alqamah: Our imam does not complete the prayer, and Alqamah said: But we complete it. He said: Meaning we pray with them and complete it."
"3683","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: People gathered on a journey, a Qurashi, an Arab, a client, a slave, and a Bedouin of the desert folk, which of them leads his companions? He said: The most learned among them in religion would lead them; if equal in learning then the best reciter; if equal in learning and recitation then the oldest. I said: If equal in learning and recitation, and the slave is the oldest, does he lead them for his age, leading the Qurashi and others? He said: Yes; why should the most learned, best reciting, and oldest among them, whoever he is, not lead them? He said: And al-Thawri used to care about it."
"3684","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed us that he heard Ibn Umar say: Salim, the client of Abu Hudhayfah, used to lead the first Muhajirun among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Ansar in the mosque of Quba, among them Abu Bakr, Umar, Abu Salamah, Zayd, and Amir ibn Rabi'ah."
"3685","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people are led by the best reciter among them; if equal in recitation then the earliest in migration; if equal in migration then the most knowledgeable of the Sunnah; if equal in knowledge then the oldest; and a man is not led in his authority, nor sat upon his cushion in his house except that he gives permission for that."
"3686","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Isma'il ibn Raja al-Zubaydi, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most entitled of the people to lead them is the best reciter of the Book of Allah; if equal in recitation then the most knowledgeable of the Sunnah; if equal in the Sunnah then the earliest in migration; if equal in migration then the oldest; and a man is not led in his authority, nor sat upon his cushion in his house unless he gives you permission."
"3687","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd al-Malik informed me, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The people are led only by the best reciter among them."
"3688","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from a man, from Amr ibn Salamah, he said: A delegation of Jarm came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered Amr ibn Salamah to lead them, though the youngest in age, because he had the most Qur'an."
"3689","From al-Thawri, from Thawr, from Muhajir ibn Damrah, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Sa'id ibn Jubayr met, and Sa'id said to Abu Salamah: Narrate, for we will follow you. Abu Salamah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When three are on a journey, let the best reciter among them lead them, even if the youngest in age; when he leads them then he is their commander. Abu Salamah said: So there is your commander whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed."
"3690","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab met riders heading to the House, and said: Who are you? The youngest among them answered him and said: Muslim servants of Allah. He said: From where have you come? He said: From the deep mountain pass. He said: Where are you heading? He said: The Ancient House. Umar said: He interpreted it, by Allah. Umar said: Who is your commander? He pointed to an elder among them, and Umar said: Rather you are their commander, to the youngest who answered him well."
"3691","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The most learned of the people, if he puts another forward instead of himself? He said: No harm in that; I myself do it."
"3692","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Amr ibn Salamah al-Jarmi, he said: A delegation came to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), taught them prayer, then said to us: Let the one with the most Qur'an lead you. So Amr ibn Salamah used to lead them though he had not reached puberty."
"3693","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The owner of the quarter leads whoever comes to him. I said to him: What is the quarter? He said: His dwelling."
"3694","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if these people, the Qurashi, the Arab, the Bedouin, the client, and the slave, each have a tent, and one of them goes to another's tent, and prayer time comes, who leads then? He said: The owner of the baggage leads them, and it is his right; he gives it to whom he wills."
"3695","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Abu Sa'id made food, then invited Abu Dharr, Hudhayfah, and Ibn Mas'ud. Prayer time came, and Abu Dharr stepped forward to lead them, but Hudhayfah said to him: Behind you; the lord of the house is more entitled to lead. Abu Dharr said to him: Is that so, O Ibn Mas'ud? He said: Yes. He said: So Abu Dharr stepped back."
"3696","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A boy who has not reached puberty visited in his quarter? He said: He leads them though he has not reached puberty, but it is said to him: It is a right; if he wishes he leads them by his right, and if he wishes he gives his right to another among them."
"3697","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: That Ibn Umar came to Makkah, and people came to him in his dwelling, and prayer time came, so he led them. When he gave salam, he said: Complete."
"3698","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Murrah al-Hamdani, he said: I came to Ibn Mas'ud seeking him in his house, and it was said: He is at Abu Musa al-Ash'ari's. So I came to him, and there were Abdullah and Hudhayfah. Abdullah said to Hudhayfah: Are you the speaker? Hudhayfah said: Yes by Allah, I said that, hating that it be said: so-and-so, and so-and-so recited it, as the Children of Israel split. He said: The prayer was established, and Abu Musa stepped forward and led them because they were in his house."
"3699","From al-Thawri and Isma'il ibn Abdullah, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'd, client of Banu Usayd, he said: I married while a slave, and invited the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Abu Dharr, Ibn Mas'ud, and Hudhayfah. Prayer time came, and Hudhayfah stepped forward to lead us, but Abu Dharr or another said to him: That is not yours. So they put me forward though a slave, and I led them."
"3700","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If the slave and the Bedouin do not recite the Qur'an, do they lead whoever comes to them in their quarter? He said: No, by my life, they do not lead. I said: If they recite only the Mother of the Qur'an? He said: I fear they have no understanding with it, and they are coarse and know nothing."
"3701","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that they used to visit A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, at the top of the valley, he and his father, Ubayd ibn Umayr, al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, and many people, and Abu Amr, the client of A'ishah, would lead them, and Abu Amr was her unemancipated boy. He was the imam of her household: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, Urwah, and their families, except Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, from whom Abu Amr would stay back. A'ishah said: When Abu Amr hides me and lowers me into my pit, he is free."
"3702","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, that A'ishah was led by her boy called Dhakwan. Ma'mar said: Ayyub said: From Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he used to lead whoever entered upon her, unless Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr entered, then he would lead her in prayer."
"3703","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim about the slave, does he lead? He said: Yes, when he establishes the prayer."
"3704","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I think he said: among the Companions of Badr whose eyesight was afflicted, used to lead their kinsfolk, among them Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, Itban ibn Malik, and Mu'adh ibn Afra."
"3705","From al-Thawri, from Abu Khalid and Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left Ibn Umm Maktum behind on the day of the Tabuk expedition, and he used to lead the people though blind."
"3706","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled, he would leave Ibn Umm Maktum in charge of Madinah."
"3707","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on an expedition and ordered Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum to lead his companions and those who stayed behind from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the chronically ill and those unable to go out."
"3708","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked about the blind man, does he lead the people? He said: Why not, if he is the most learned among them? A man said to Ata: Except that he mistakes the qiblah? Ata said: If he mistakes, they should correct him; let him lead them if he is the most learned among them."
"3709","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim about the blind man, does he lead? He said: Yes, when he establishes the prayer."
"3710","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn Abbas said: How do I lead them when they direct me to the qiblah, after he went blind?"
"3711","From his father, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn Abbas led them in a single garment, though blind, on a mat covering the house."
"3712","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: He used to lead them though blind."
"3713","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa asked Ata about the child of adultery, if agreeable, does he lead the people? He said: Yes. Sulayman said: And we hold that."
"3714","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar saw no harm in that."
"3715","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim about the child of adultery, the Bedouin, the slave, and the blind, do they lead? He said: Yes, when they establish the prayer."
"3716","From al-Thawri, from Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi say: The child of adultery marries and is married to, his testimony is valid, and he leads."
"3717","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri: Does the child of adultery lead? He said: Yes, and what is wrong with him? I said: And the effeminate? He said: No, nor honor; he is not followed."
"3718","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: A man does not lead his father, nor his brother older than him."
"3719","Ma'mar reported, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I was with Anas ibn Malik, and he came out from his land heading to Basra, between it and Basra three miles or three leagues. Prayer time came, and he put forward a son of his called Abu Bakr, who led us in Fajr prayer and recited Surah Tabarak. When he finished, he said to him: You made it long for us."
"3720","From Sa'id ibn Qumadiz, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman: That al-Zubayr used to pray behind his son Abdullah."
"3721","From Ma'mar, that Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr used to lead al-Zubayr and Talhah. He said: And Abu Bakr used to lead his father."
"3722","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The boy who has not reached puberty does not lead."
"3723","Al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: That he disliked the boy leading until he reaches puberty."
"3724","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The boy does not lead until he reaches puberty, and let your best call the adhan for you."
"3725","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim informed me that Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed him that Muhammad ibn Abi Suwayd set him up for the people, though a boy, at Ta'if in the month of Ramadan, leading them. He wrote about that to Umar giving glad tidings, but Umar grew angry and wrote to him: Your share was not to put forward for the people a boy upon whom the legal penalties are not yet due."
"3726","From Ma'mar, that al-Dahhak ibn Qays ordered a boy before he reached puberty, and he led the people in prayer. It was said to him: Why did you do that? Al-Dahhak said: He has of the Qur'an what I do not have; I only put the Qur'an forward. Ma'mar said: And it reached me that the imam in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray though he had not reached puberty, and he had the most Qur'an."
"3727","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: The Arabs used to say: Look at this one, what will he and his people do? Meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Makkah, delegations of people came to him. There was a boy of Jarm called Amr ibn Salamah; whenever one of those who delegated to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him, he learned Qur'an from him. He said: And he had the most Qur'an of his people, so he used to lead them though a child not yet pubescent, wearing a worn waist-wrapper. An old woman of the quarter would say: Will you not clothe your imam? He said: So they bought me a waist-wrapper for three dirhams. He said: So I rejoiced in it greatly."
"3728","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Nafi' informed me, he said: Prayer was established in a mosque in a quarter of Madinah. He said: Abdullah ibn Umar had land near that mosque which he worked. The imam of that mosque's people was a client, and that client's and his companions' dwelling was there. When Abdullah ibn Umar heard them and they established the prayer, he came to witness the prayer with them. The client, the owner of the mosque, said to Ibn Umar: Come forward and lead. Abdullah said: You are more entitled to pray in your mosque. So the client led."
"3729","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A traveler passing by people at a water source, prayer time comes, and they put him forward, though they have no imam; does he lead them? He said: No harm in that."
"3730","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: A group gathered at one of the waters around Makkah, he said: I think he said: at the top of the valley here, he said: during Hajj. Prayer time came, and a man from the family of Abu al-Sa'ib al-Makhzumi, foreign-tongued, stepped forward. Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah put him back and put another forward. Umar ibn al-Khattab was absent and he did not inform him of anything until he came to Madinah. When he came to Madinah he informed him of that. Al-Miswar said: Wait for me, O Commander of the Believers; the man was foreign-tongued, and it was during Hajj, so I feared some pilgrims would hear his recitation and take it for his foreignness. He said: Is that where you were headed? He said: Yes. He said: You were right."
"3731","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man visited in his quarter leading the people, and he recites something of the Qur'an mixed with rhymed prose, he said: Let him not lead you; do not pray with them. And if he mixes Qur'an from here and there, then pray with his prayer."
"3732","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, that Ibn Mas'ud passed by people at a water source while prayer had been established. He joined them, and a man among them led them. He recited and added in his recitation: We perform Hajj to our Lord's House and fulfill the debt. And other than Qatadah added: And they are like sandgrouse swooping. Ibn Mas'ud said: We never heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but fabrication. He said: The Bedouin stepped back, and Ibn Mas'ud stepped forward and led them."
"3733","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from a man of Tayyi', he said: Ibn Mas'ud passed by one of our mosques, and a man among them stepped forward and recited the Opening of the Book, then said: We perform Hajj to our Lord's House and fulfill the debt, and it is like swooping sandgrouse. Abdullah said: We never heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but fabrication. He said: Abdullah left."
"3734","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I was informed that Humayd ibn al-Himyari said: Ibn Mas'ud prayed behind the Bedouin. The Bedouin recited the Mother of the Qur'an, and when he finished it and said: Not of those who earned anger nor of those astray, he said: We perform Hajj to our Lord's House and fulfill the debt for it, like swooping sandgrouse. Ibn Mas'ud said: We never heard of this in the latter religion; this is nothing but fabrication. He said: The Bedouin stepped back until Ibn Mas'ud stepped forward, knowing he was more learned than him. Ibn Mas'ud said: I never saw a Bedouin more learned than him."
"3735","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is it not that if the imam wishes to lead the people in what the voice is raised in of recitation, he raises with the Mother of the Qur'an in every rak'ah, never adding to it? He said: Yes, and more beloved to me is that he raise with them with a surah."
"3736","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Was the imam commanded to raise the voice with the Qur'an? He said: Yes, and al-Zubayr used to raise his voice with recitation until his recitation had a loud din in the mosque. I said: What if a man, an imam, added nothing but making them hear something? He said: That suffices him."
"3737","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father, he said: Umar's recitation in the dawn prayer could be heard from the house of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas."
"3738","From Malik, from his uncle Abu Suhayl ibn Malik, from his father, he said: Umar's recitation could be heard from al-Balat."
"3739","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I spent a night at my aunt Maymunah's. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying voluntary night prayer. He went to the waterskin and performed ablution, then stood praying. When I saw him do that I got up, performed ablution from the waterskin, then stood to the left side. He took my hand behind his back and moved me likewise from behind his back to the right side. I said: Was that in voluntary prayer? He said: Yes."
"3740","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I slept at my aunt Maymunah bint al-Harith's. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose at night, relieved himself, then came and washed his face and hands, then slept. He said: Then he rose praying at night, went to the waterskin and performed ablution between two ablutions, not excessive yet thorough, then stood and prayed. He said: And I stretched as if reluctant to be seen watching him, meaning observing him, then got up and did as he did. I stood to his left, and he kept moving my ear until he turned me, and I was to his right while he prayed. He said: His prayer completed thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then he lay down and slept until he snored. Then Bilal came and announced the prayer to him, so he rose and prayed without performing ablution. And Yahya added to me in this hadith, from al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, he said: In his supplication was: O Allah, place light in my heart, light in my hearing, light in my sight, light to my right, light to my left, light above me, light beneath me, light before me, light behind me, and magnify light for me. Kurayb said: And six with me in the chest: my sinew, my brain, my blood, my hair, my skin, and my bones."
"3741","From al-Thawri, he said: It was mentioned to us from Ibn Abbas, that it was mentioned to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept, and he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was guarded. One of the jurists said that he said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eye sleeps but his heart does not sleep."
"3742","From Malik, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My two eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep."
"3743","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Sumay' al-Zayyat, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I stood to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he turned me and placed me to his right. Sufyan said: In voluntary prayer."
"3744","From Malik, from Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, that Ibn Abbas informed him that he spent the night at his aunt Maymunah's. He said: I lay across the pillow, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wife lay along it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept until midnight, or a little before or after, then woke and sat, wiped sleep from his face with his hands, then recited the last ten verses of Surah Al Imran. Then he went to a hanging waterskin, performed ablution from it excellently, then stood praying. I did the like of what he did, then went and stood beside him. He placed his hands on my head and took my ear twisting it. Then he prayed two rak'ahs, then two, then two, then two, then prayed witr. He lay down until the caller came, then rose and prayed two light rak'ahs, then went out and prayed dawn."
"3745","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I prayed with my father, and his wife stood behind us."
"3746","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I was in Maymunah's house. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose praying at night, and I stood with him to his left. He took my hand and placed me to his right, then prayed thirteen rak'ahs. I estimated his standing in each rak'ah as: O you enwrapped."
"3747","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi', client of Ibn Umar, informed me that he stood alone to the left of Ibn Umar, who pulled him with his right until he pulled him to his right side."
"3748","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If a man prays with another man, where is he from him? He said: Likewise to his right side. I said: Does he align with him so lined with him, neither outstripping the other? He said: Yes. He said: I said: Must he cling to him so no gap between them? He said: Yes, by Allah then."
"3749","From Ma'mar, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I prayed with Anas ibn Malik, and he placed me to his right, while Jamilah, his umm walad, stood behind us."
"3750","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah about two men with a woman, he said: The man stands to his companion's right, and the woman stands behind them. From al-Thawri, the like of Qatadah's saying."
"3751","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The man stands at the imam's corner, and the two women behind them. I said: And women? He said: Likewise also; the man at the man's corner, and the women behind them."
"3752","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me from Ikrimah, he said: Ibn Abbas said: I prayed beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while A'ishah behind us prayed with us, and I beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying with him."
"3753","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: One of the two men stands behind the other, and the woman behind them."
"3754","From Malik, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas ibn Malik, from his grandmother Mulaykah, meaning Ishaq's grandmother, that she invited the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to food she made. He ate, then said: Stand, let me pray for you. He said: So I went to a mat of ours blackened from long use, sprinkled it with water. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, and I and the orphan lined behind him, and the old woman behind us. He prayed two rak'ahs for us, then left."
"3755","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if they are three? He said: People say: Two stand at his corner, and another stands behind him. He said: I said: What do you say? He said: I say three is a congregation; when three, one of them should lead them, and two should stand behind, so let them stand."
"3756","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar said: They two pray behind him."
"3757","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Umar said: When three, he places two men behind him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, the like of Ibn Umar's saying."
"3758","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: Three is a congregation."
"3759","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Alqamah and al-Aswad came with Ibn Mas'ud to a mosque. The people met them having prayed, so he took them up to the house. He placed one to his right and the other to his left, then led them in prayer."
"3760","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, that Abdullah led Alqamah and al-Aswad in prayer. This one stood to his right and this one to his left, then he stood between them."
"3761","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: When three, let them all line together; when more than that, let one of them step forward."
"3762","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Three is a congregation. And Hisham mentioned it from al-Hasan also."
"3763","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan about an imam whom prayer overtakes with only one man, he said: He places him to his right; when a third comes, he steps back and they stand behind him."
"3764","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from his father, he said: I entered upon Umar ibn al-Khattab while he was praying voluntary prayer in the midday heat. He placed me aligned to his right, and it stayed so until Yarfa', his client, entered. Then I stepped back to the rows, and we lined behind Umar."
"3765","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah informed me that his father Abdullah ibn Utbah entered upon Umar and found him praying voluntary prayer. He stood to his left, and Umar moved him back to his right. Then Yarfa', Umar's client, came, and I stepped back with him, and I and Yarfa' prayed behind him."
"3766","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When prayer is established and none in the mosque but the imam and a man, he stands behind him as between him and the bow; if someone comes, otherwise he steps forward to his right. He said: And al-Sha'bi said: He stands to his right, and al-Sha'bi's saying is more beloved to Sufyan."
"3767","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: I used to stand behind al-Aswad until the caller descended."
"3768","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: He stands to his right."
"3769","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever leads people while they dislike him, his prayer does not pass his collarbone."
"3770","From al-Thawri and Isma'il, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, from Ibrahim, he said: I used to stand behind Alqamah until the caller descended."
"3771","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever leads a people in prayer while they dislike him, his prayer does not pass beyond his collarbone. From Ma'mar, from Qatada, from al-Hasan, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the same."
"3772","From Isma'il, or another, that Shurayh used to lead his people in prayer, and when the affair of Hujr ibn Adi happened, they suspected Shurayh in his matter, and when he stepped forward to pray with them, they said: Step back. He said: Are all of you of this view? They said: Yes. So Shurayh stepped back."
"3773","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Amra, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr praying in the niche of the imam, he said: And I saw Ma'mar, when he led us, praying in the niche of the imam."
"3774","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he used to dislike praying in the niche of the imam. From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same. Al-Thawri said: And we dislike it."
"3775","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw al-Hasan come to Thabit al-Bunani, I think he was visiting him, and prayer came, so Thabit said: Come forward, O Abu Sa'id. Al-Hasan said: You, for you are more entitled. Thabit said: By Allah, I will never go before you. So al-Hasan stepped forward, and kept away from the niche, not praying in it. Ibn al-Taymi said: And I saw my father, and Layth, keeping away from it."
"3776","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, he said: I heard al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim saying: The first association in this misguidance was these prayer niches."
"3777","From al-Thawri, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ubayd ibn Abi al-Ja'd al-Ashja'i, from Ka'b, he said: In the end times there will be a people whose lifespans are shortened, and they will adorn their mosques, and take in them altars like the altars of the Christians, and when they do that, trial will be poured upon them."
"3778","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Mujahid, he said: Sulayman saw Hudhayfa leading them on a bench of plaster, so he said: Step back, for you are only a man of the people, so do not raise yourself above them. He said: You spoke the truth."
"3779","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, or another, Abu Bakr doubted, that Ibn Mas'ud, or he said: Abu Mas'ud, I doubt, and Sulayman, and Hudhayfa, one of them prayed with them, then he went to pray on a bench, so his two companions pulled him and said: Come down from it."
"3780","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah, from Shu'ba, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: Ibn Mas'ud came to our mosque, and prayer was established, so it was said to him: Come forward. He said: Let your imam lead you. It was said to him: The imam is not here. He said: Let a man of you step forward. So he stepped forward, and wanted to stand on something like a bench, so Abd Allah forbade him."
"3781","Utba ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith told us, that Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, told him, that he saw Ibn Abbas praying in the screened enclosure with Mu'awiya."
"3782","Al-Thawri told us, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Hudhali, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik praying with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz in the screened enclosure."
"3783","Ma'mar told us, he said: One who saw Anas and al-Hasan told me they pray in the screened enclosure, he said: And I myself saw Ma'mar praying in the screened enclosure."
"3784","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw al-Hasan in the screened enclosure praying more than once, nodding with his head then standing and praying, and not performing ablution."
"3785","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf al-Dhayyak, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about the screened enclosure, so he said: They only did that fearing that they would stab them."
"3786","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that al-Ahnaf ibn Qays would not pray in the screened enclosure, and would say: It is a sanctuary. And he would not sleep in the pavilion, and would say: The pavilion was mentioned only for the people of the Fire."
"3787","From a man, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he used to dislike praying in the screened enclosure. He said: And Hammad said: The first row is that which is next to the screened enclosure."
"3788","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: Whoever prays the obligatory prayer, then wants to do voluntary prayer, let him speak, or let him walk, and let him pray ahead of that. He said: And Ibn Abbas said: I say to the slave girl: See how much of the night has gone? Only to separate between them."
"3789","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: One who saw Ibn Umar told me, and a man prayed the obligatory prayer then stood in his place in which he prayed the obligatory doing voluntary prayer in it, so Ibn Umar pushed him, and when he finished, Ibn Umar said to him: Do you know why I pushed you? He said: No, except that I think you only pushed me for good. He said: Yes, because you did not speak since you finished the obligatory, and you did not pray ahead of you."
"3790","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar told me, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, he told him, he said: I prayed Friday with Mu'awiya in the screened enclosure, and when he gave salam I stood in my place and prayed, and when he entered he sent to me, and said: Do not repeat what you did, when you pray Friday do not join it until you speak or leave, for the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered that."
"3791","From al-Thawri, from Maysara ibn Habib al-Nahdi, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abbad ibn Abd Allah al-Asadi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: It is not proper for the imam to pray voluntary prayer in the place in which he led the people until he moves, or separates with speech."
"3792","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When one of you prays the obligatory prayer then wants to do voluntary with something, let him move forward a little, or move back a little, or to his right, or to his left."
"3793","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: I heard Mansur narrating, from Ibrahim, he said: The imam does not pray voluntary prayer where he prays the obligatory prayer."
"3794","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, that he saw a people praying in the mosque, and when they finished they stepped back to pray after the obligatory, so he said: They used to step forward and not step back."
"3795","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: I mentioned to Ibn al-Musayyab, that Ibn Umar saw a man praying on Friday in his place as voluntary, so Ibn Umar forbade him that, and said: I do not see you praying in your place. Ibn al-Musayyab said: That was disliked only for the imam."
"3796","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he used to lead them, then do voluntary in his place, he said: And when he prayed the obligatory he glorified Allah in his place. From al-Thawri, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"3797","From al-Thawri, from a shaykh of ours called Abu Bahr, from a shaykh of theirs, he said: Abd Allah came to us, so we wanted to put him forward, so he said: One of you should step forward. And Abd Allah was asked about a man praying the obligatory, may he do voluntary in his place? So he said: Yes."
"3798","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mis'ar, from a man, from his father, that Ibn Mas'ud saw no harm in that."
"3799","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim, he said: It was said to Tawus: Does a man move when he prays the obligatory from his place to do voluntary? So Tawus said: Are you teaching Allah your religion?"
"3800","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to dislike one leading them while he reads in the mushaf, imitating the People of the Book."
"3801","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, that he disliked it."
"3802","From Abd al-Quddus ibn Habib Abu Sa'id, from al-Hasan, he said: I heard him saying: There is no harm in a man leading in Ramadan while he reads in the mushaf."
"3803","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that A'isha used to read in the mushaf while she was praying."
"3804","Ma'mar told us, from Ayyub, he said: Ibn Sirin would pray with the mushaf beside him, and when he hesitated he looked in it."
"3805","From Ibn Jurayj, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Mihjan al-Du'ili, from his father, he said: I prayed Zuhr and Asr in my house, then I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and sat with him, then prayer was established, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed and I did not pray, and when he finished, he said: Are you not a Muslim? I said: Yes. He said: What is with you that you did not pray? He said: I said: I prayed in my house. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: When prayer is established, then pray, even if you had prayed."
"3806","Ma'mar told us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Busr ibn Mihjan, from his father, he said: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and spoke to him for a need, then prayer was established while I was sitting, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed with the people then finished, and found me sitting, so he said to me: Are you not a Muslim? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: What prevented you from praying with us? He said: I said: I prayed in my lodging. He said: Even if you had prayed in your lodging."
"3807","From Hisham ibn Hassan, and al-Thawri, from Ya'la ibn Ata al-Ta'ifi, from Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad al-Khuza'i, from his father, he said: We prayed Fajr with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then he turned and saw two men behind the people, so he called them, and they were brought with their limbs trembling, so he said: What prevented you from praying with the people? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we prayed in our lodgings. He said: Do not do so, when one of you prays in his lodging, then reaches prayer with the imam, let him pray them with him, for it is voluntary for him."
"3808","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Sa'id ibn Ubayd, from Sila ibn Zufar al-Absi, he said: I went out with Hudhayfa, and he passed a mosque, so he prayed Maghrib with them and added a rak'a, though he had prayed."
"3809","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When I pray the obligatory then catch it with the people, I make what I prayed in my house voluntary, and I make my prayer with the imam the obligatory. I said: What if you catch only one rak'a? He said: Likewise also."
"3810","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: I asked him about Asr, should he repeat it when the congregation comes? Ma'mar said, Ibn al-Musayyab said: Pray with the people, for your prayer with them exceeds your prayer alone by twenty-four prayers, or some twenty prayers."
"3811","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: A man asked Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: I prayed in my house, then I came and found the people praying, which of them shall I make my prayer? He said: And is that up to you? That is only up to Allah."
"3812","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' told me, that Ibn Umar said: If you had prayed among your family, then reached the prayer in the mosque with the imam, then pray with him, except the Subh prayer and the Maghrib prayer, which is called the Isha prayer, for they are not prayed twice."
"3813","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: When he prayed in his house, then went out and found the imam praying, he prayed with him, except Subh and Maghrib."
"3814","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba: that he used to dislike repeating Maghrib in congregation."
"3815","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, he said: Repeat all the prayers except Asr and Fajr, and he says: your first prayer of them is the one that counts."
"3816","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he was asked about Maghrib which a man prays in his house, then finds the people in it? He said: Make odd what you prayed in your house with a rak'a, then give salam, and join the people, and make the one they are in the obligatory."
"3817","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I prayed the last Isha and prayed Witr, then I entered the mosque while the imam was in the last rak'a, so I went to make it even with intercession, but I did not finish until the imam bowed and rose from the last rak'a. He said: Do not repeat, but you have prayed Witr."
"3818","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I prayed alone one rak'a then they stood, and I fear I cannot join my rak'a with a rak'a until they finish, shall I pray with them? He said: Rather join it with a rak'a, then leave and pray with them."
"3819","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: He used to say: When you leave your house, you are in prayer."
"3820","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I heard that voluntary prayer is disliked in midday until the sun declines, and when sunrise comes, and when sunset comes, he said: It reached me that it rises between the two horns of Satan, and sets between his two horns."
"3821","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit told me, that Abu Umama asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: What are you? He said: A Prophet. He said: To whom were you sent? He said: To the red and the black. He said: At what time is prayer disliked? He said: From when you pray Subh until the sun rises a spear length, and from when the sun yellows to its setting. He said: Which supplication is most heard? He said: The last half of the night, and after the obligatory prayers. He said: When is sunset? He said: From the first of the sun yellowing when yellowness enters it until the sun sets."
"3822","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from a man, from Ka'b ibn Murra al-Bahzi, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which night is most heard? He said: The last depth of the night. He said: Then prayer is accepted until dawn rises, then no prayer until the sun is a spear length, or two spears, then prayer is accepted until the sun sets."
"3823","Ma'mar told us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Abd Allah al-Sunabihi, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The sun rises between the two horns of Satan, or he said: there rises with it a horn of Satan, and when it rises high it leaves it, and when it is in the middle of the sky it joins it, and when it declines, or he said: passes the zenith, it leaves it, and when it nears setting it joins it, so do not pray these three hours."
"3824","From Malik, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let none of you intend to pray at sunrise, nor at its setting."
"3825","From Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Umar, that he used to say: Do not intend sunrise, nor its setting, for Satan rises his two horns with its rising, and they set with its setting. He said: And Umar used to beat men over them."
"3826","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Tawus, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, only said: Do not intend sunrise, nor its setting, in prayer, so we do not intend it."
"3827","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from one who heard Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This prayer which was imposed on those before you, meaning Asr, they neglected it, so whoever guards it today has its reward twice, and no prayer after it until the witness is seen, and the witness is the star."
"3828","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Abd Allah said: I do not like the prayer of a man when the sun reddens, or he said: yellows, for two fils, until it rises a palm height."
"3829","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from A'isha, that she said: Look at these who left prayer, until when it is the hour in which prayer is disliked they stand praying. He said: And that is when the storyteller Bukra stood. Ata said: I think when sunrise came."
"3830","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Prayer is disliked in three hours, and forbidden in two hours, he said: It is disliked after Asr, and after Subh until it rises a palm height, and midday in severe heat, and forbidden in two hours when the horn of Satan rises until its rising is complete, and when it yellows until its setting is complete, for it sets in the horn of Satan, and rises in the horn of Satan. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, the same."
"3831","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: No prayer after the Asr prayer until the sun sets, and no prayer after the Subh prayer until the sun rises."
"3832","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar told me, from Abd Allah ibn Iyad, and from Ata ibn Bukht, both from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that they heard him saying: I heard Abu al-Qasim, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: No prayer after the Subh prayer until the sun rises, and no prayer after the Asr prayer until night. Abd Allah ibn Iyad said to him: Ibn al-Zubayr prays after Asr, and before sunrise, among youths, so Abu Sa'id said to him: He used to blame that on the people, meaning Banu Umayya."
"3833","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from Qaza'a, he said: I used to pray two rak'as after Asr, so Abu Sa'id al-Khudri met me and forbade me them, so he said: Shall I leave them for you? He said: Yes."
"3834","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Khubayb ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Asim, from Abu Hurayra, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade prayer in two hours, after Asr until the sun sets, and after Subh until the sun rises."
"3835","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr praying after Asr two rak'as, so I said: What is this? He said: A'isha told me: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray after Asr two rak'as. He said: So I went to A'isha and asked her, so she said: He spoke the truth. So I said: I bear witness I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: No prayer after Asr until the sun sets, nor after Fajr until the sun rises. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, does what he was ordered, and we do what we were ordered."
"3836","From Ma'mar, from Abu Harun al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I saw Umar beating over them the tops of the mountains, meaning two rak'as after Asr."
"3837","From al-Thawri, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, he said: Umar struck al-Munkadir when he saw him praying voluntary after Asr."
"3838","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab beating over prayer after Asr."
"3839","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr saying: Abu Ghadiya told me, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab beating the people over the two rak'as after Asr."
"3840","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Ali prayed voluntary on a journey after Asr two rak'as, so Umar was angry with him, and said: By Allah, you know that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to forbid this."
"3841","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', he said: I said to him: I saw Ibn Umar praying on the Day of Sacrifice in the early day? He said: No, nor on other than the Day of Sacrifice until the sun rises. He said: And Ibn Umar used to say: As for me, I pray as I saw my companions praying, and as for me, I do not forbid anyone to pray night or day, not intending sunrise nor sunset, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade that, and said: The horn of Satan rises with sunrise, so let no one intend sunrise nor sunset."
"3842","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that A'isha and Umm Salama used to pray two rak'as after Asr."
"3843","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pray after Asr at all, except once people came to him after Zuhr and busied him in something, and he did not pray after Zuhr anything until he prayed Asr. She said: When he prayed Asr he entered my house, and prayed two rak'as."
"3844","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Labid, he said: I heard Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman saying: Mu'awiya came to Medina, and said: Stand, O Kathir ibn al-Salt, to the Mother of the Believers, and ask her about the two rak'as after Asr. Abu Salama said: So I stood with him, and Ibn Abbas sent Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, so we came to A'isha, and she said: I do not know, ask Umm Salama. So we came to Umm Salama, and she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon us one day, and prayed two rak'as after Asr, which I had not seen him praying, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, what are these two rak'as? He said: A delegation of Banu Tamim came, or he said: charity came, and I used to pray two rak'as after Zuhr, and I had not prayed them, so these are they."
"3845","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I heard Abu Sa'd the blind telling from a man called al-Sa'ib, freedman of the Persians, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that Umar ibn al-Khattab saw him, while a caliph, bowing after Asr two rak'as, so he walked to him and struck him with the stick while he was praying as he is, and when he finished, Zayd said: Strike, O Commander of the Faithful, for by Allah I will never leave them after I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying them. He said: So Umar sat to him, and said: O Zayd ibn Khalid, were I not afraid that people would take them as a ladder to prayer until night, I would not have struck for them."
"3846","From Abd Allah ibn Sa'id, he said: al-Azraq ibn Qays told me, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Rabah al-Ansari narrating, from a man of the Ansar, of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed Asr, then a man stood praying after it, so Umar ibn al-Khattab took his cloak, or his garment, and said: Sit, for the People of the Book before you perished only because their prayer had no separation. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Ibn al-Khattab spoke the truth."
"3847","From Hushaym, or another, he said: Abu Hamza told me, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about prayer after Asr, so he said: Pray what you wish till night. He said: And I saw Umar beating the man he sees praying after Asr."
"3848","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn al-Mus'ab told me, that Tawus told him, that he asked Ibn Abbas about two rak'as after Asr, so he forbade him them, so I said: I will not leave them. So Ibn Abbas said: And it is not for a believing man nor a believing woman, when Allah and His Messenger decree a matter, and he recited this verse to clear."
"3849","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysara told me, that Tawus made him stand at Khayf of Mina between him and the people, to pray after Asr two rak'as, he said: So he prayed two rak'as, and said to me: Do you pray after Asr? He said: I was compelled, by Allah."
"3850","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari used to pray before the caliphate of Umar two rak'as after Asr, and when Umar became caliph he left them, and when he died he bowed them, so it was said to him: What is this? So he said: Umar used to beat the people over them. Ibn Tawus said: And my father would not leave them."
"3851","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Urwa ibn al-Zubayr mentioning that Urwa told him, that A'isha told him that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, never entered upon her but bowed after Asr two rak'as."
"3852","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, he said: We used to pray with Ibn al-Zubayr Asr in the Sacred Mosque, and he would pray after Asr two rak'as, and we would pray them with him, standing in a row behind him."
"3853","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Anas, that he was asked about two rak'as before Maghrib, he said: I saw the elite of the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praying them."
"3854","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to pray the two rak'as before Maghrib."
"3855","From Hushaym, he said: Ya'la ibn Ata told us, from Thumama ibn Abd Allah ibn Anas, he said: People of the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to pray the two rak'as before Maghrib."
"3856","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was narrated to me from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to come out to us after the sun set, when it is night, and before the call for Maghrib, while we were praying, and he would not forbid us nor order us."
"3857","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The Emigrants would not bow the two rak'as before Maghrib, and the Helpers would bow them. Al-Zuhri said: And Anas would bow them."
"3858","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman did not pray the two rak'as before Maghrib."
"3859","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Amir, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I saw the elite of the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when Maghrib was called they hastened to the pillars to pray two rak'as before Maghrib."
"3860","From Ibn Jurayj, and al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Ata ibn Yasar told him, that he heard Abu Hurayra saying: When prayer is established, there is no prayer except the obligatory."
"3861","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Hasan ibn Musafir, from Suwayd ibn Ghafala, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab used to beat over prayer after the iqama."
"3862","Ma'mar told us, from Bala', from Ayyub, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayra, raising it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: When prayer is established, there is no prayer except the obligatory."
"3863","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, that Safwan ibn Mawhab told him, that he heard Muslim ibn Aqil saying to the people while they were praying: though prayer has been established, woe to you, there is no prayer when prayer is established."
"3864","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: One who heard Muslim ibn Aqil told me, forbidding prayer after the iqama."
"3865","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, he said: I asked Tawus, I said: Shall I bow two rak'as while the mu'adhdhin is calling the iqama? He said: Can you bear that?"
"3866","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, and Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that they both disliked prayer at the iqama. And Ibrahim said: If you have entered something, complete it."
"3867","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When prayer is established, there is no prayer, and if the imam comes out while you are bowing, join to it another light rak'a, then give salam."
"3868","Abu Sa'id, doubting, narrated it from al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by Ibn al-Ushb, while he was praying two rak'as when prayer was established, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Two prayers together?"
"3869","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The imam came out, while I am doing voluntary, shall I complete? He said: Join it with it. I said: I did not give the salam of finishing. He said: Have you not recited tashahhud? I said: Yes. He said: That suffices you, so join it with it."
"3870","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: I was standing praying, then I passed a prostration of the Quran, so I fell prostrate in that prostration, he said: Join it with it. I said: Shall I say takbir? He said: Yes. I said: Shall I seek refuge? He said: Yes. I said: Does my seeking refuge for the voluntary not suffice me? He said: Yes, but I prefer that you seek refuge."
"3871","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd Allah ibn Kathir al-Razi told me, that Nafi' ibn Jubayr used to join the voluntary with the obligatory, he said: So I recognized it, he said: It is not his own view."
"3872","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, and Qatada: that they both used to do that, joining the voluntary with the obligatory."
"3873","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, and al-A'mash, and al-Zubayr, from Ibrahim, about a man entering a mosque thinking they had prayed, so he prayed two rak'as of the obligatory, then prayer was established, he said: He enters with the imam and prays two rak'as, then gives salam, then makes the remaining two voluntary. Al-Zubayr said: So I said to Ibrahim: I did not know anyone does this. He said: This is what those before you used to do."
"3874","From al-Thawri, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He cuts his prayer, and enters with the people."
"3875","From al-Thawri, from one who heard Ibrahim saying: When you enter a prayer, do not enter another with it, meaning: when you are in the obligatory, do not make it voluntary, or in voluntary do not make it obligatory."
"3876","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, that he said: When you join the voluntary with the obligatory, it is like speech, meaning: but give salam and enter with them. Ma'mar said, and al-Hasan said it."
"3877","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Maysara, from Suwayd ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Sahla ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out while the mu'adhdhin was calling the iqama, so he prayed Fajr, and found two men praying, so he said: Two prayers together?"
"3878","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a man praying while the mu'adhdhin was calling the iqama for Subh, so he said: Do you pray Subh four?"
"3879","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar saw a man praying while the mu'adhdhin was calling the iqama, so he said: Do you pray Subh four? Ma'mar said: And it reached me from Sa'id ibn Jubayr the same."
"3880","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu al-Aliya, or from Abu Uthman, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a man praying two rak'as while the Fajr prayer had been established, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Which is your prayer? The one you prayed alone, or the one you prayed with us?"
"3881","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he disliked praying them at the iqama, he said: How shall he pray them when prayer has been made obligatory?"
"3882","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I entered the mosque while the imam is in prayer and I had not bowed them, he said: Bow them in the mosque, unless you fear the rak'a the imam is in will escape you."
"3883","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you prefer that I bow them on the road? He said: I do not care where you bow them, if you bow them before the prayer."
"3884","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I fear Fajr will escape me? He said: Leave them, and let nothing of Subh escape you. He said: Then I told him the report of Abu Sa'd the blind to us from the one who bowed them after Subh in the time of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"3885","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I bow them in my house then come to the mosque and sit, more beloved to me. Zayd ibn Khalid said: Do not make your houses graves."
"3886","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If you miss bowing them before Subh, bow them after Subh."
"3887","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When prayer is established and you have not bowed the two rak'as of Fajr, pray with the imam, and when he finishes, bow them after Subh."
"3888","And I saw Ibn Jurayj bowing them after Subh in the mosque of San'a after the imam gave salam."
"3889","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, brother of Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrating, from his grandfather, he said: He went out to Subh, then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered Subh, and he had not bowed the two rak'as of Fajr, so he prayed with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then stood when he finished Subh, and bowed the two rak'as of Fajr. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by him, and said: What is this prayer? So he told him, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was silent and went on and did not say anything."
"3890","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he entered the mosque while the people were in prayer, and he had not prayed the two rak'as of Fajr, so he entered with the people in their prayer, then sat, until when the sun rose for him he made them up. He said: And when the iqama was called while he was on the roads, he would pray them on the road."
"3891","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan told me, from one who told him, from Ibn Umar, that he bowed in Duha two rak'as, while he never prayed the Duha prayer at all, so it was said to him: We never saw you praying this prayer at all. He said: I had forgotten the two rak'as of Fajr, so I bowed them now."
"3892","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar, while he was dressing for Subh, heard the iqama, so he prayed in the chamber the two rak'as of Fajr, then went out and prayed with the people. He said: And when Ibn Umar found the imam praying and he had not bowed them, he would enter with the imam, then pray them after sunrise."
"3893","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa told me, he said: It reached us, from Abu al-Darda, that he used to say: Yes, by Allah, if I enter while the people are in prayer, I shall go to a pillar of the pillars of the mosque, then bow them then complete them, then not hasten from completing them, then walk to the people and pray with the people Subh."
"3894","From Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa, he said: Ibn Mas'ud came to us while the imam was praying Fajr, so he prayed two rak'as at a pillar, while he had not prayed the two rak'as of Fajr. From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa, from Ibn Mas'ud, the same. From Ma'mar, he said: And al-Hasan used to do it."
"3895","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, and Asim, from al-Sha'bi: that Masruq used to pray them while the imam was standing praying in the mosque."
"3896","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, he said: When you enter the mosque while the imam is in prayer, and you have not bowed the two rak'as of Fajr, pray them then enter with the imam. Hisham said: And Ibn Umar, and al-Nakha'i would enter with the imam, and not bow then."
"3897","From al-Thawri, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If he does not make up the two rak'as of Fajr, there is nothing on him."
"3898","Ma'mar told us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stand in the Fajr prayer when he raised his head from bowing, he said: Our Lord, and to You is praise, in the last rak'a, he said: O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so, supplicating against people of the hypocrites. He said: So Allah revealed: You have nothing of the matter, whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers."
"3899","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayra, he said: When he raised his head from the last rak'a in the Fajr prayer, he said: O Allah, our Lord, and to You is praise, O Allah, save al-Walid ibn al-Walid, and Salama ibn Hisham, and Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'a, and the oppressed of the believers in Mecca, O Allah, make severe Your grip on Mudar, and make it upon them like the years of Yusuf."
"3900","Ma'mar told us, from Asim, from Anas, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, performed qunut for a month in the Subh prayer, supplicating against tribes of Arab tribes, Usayya, Dhakwan, Ri'l, and Lihyan, and all of them of Banu Sulaym."
"3901","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from a man of the people of Ta'if, he said: A dog came, while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was praying with the people the Asr prayer, to pass in front of them, so a man of the people said: O Allah, restrain it, so the dog died, and when the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, finished, he said: Which of you supplicated against it? So a man said: I, O Messenger of Allah. So he said: Had he supplicated against a nation, it would have been answered for him."
"3902","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr told me, he said: Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'a, and Salama ibn Hisham, and al-Walid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughira fled from the idolaters to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with Ayyash and Salama fettered, riding together on a camel, and al-Walid driving them, then the finger of al-Walid was wounded, so he said: Are you but a finger that bled, and in the way of Allah what you met. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, knew of their coming to him and their affair, before the people knew, so he prayed Subh and bowed in the first rak'a of them, and when he raised his head he supplicated for them before prostrating, so he said: O Allah, save Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'a, O Allah, save Salama ibn Hisham, O Allah, save al-Walid son of al-Walid, O Allah, save the oppressed of the believers, O Allah, make severe Your grip on Mudar, and make them upon them years like the years of Yusuf."
"3903","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: I supplicated in the obligatory against a man and named him by his name, he said: Your prayer is cut. Then he told me then, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, supplicated for Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'a and bowed, and when he raised his head from the rak'a, he said while standing: O Allah, save Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'a, and al-Walid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughira, and Salama ibn Hisham, and the oppressed of Your servants. I said: So he supplicated with this and named what he named. He said: I do not know if it was in a voluntary or an obligatory. I said: What if the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, supplicated for them in the obligatory? He said: I do not know, and perhaps the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was ordered with that, and we are not like him. Ibn Jurayj said: Ata said: He supplicated for them then did not supplicate after that, as far as reached me."
"3904","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: A man supplicates in the obligatory, seeking forgiveness of his Lord and asking Him, he said: I do not like it. I said: Does that cut his prayer? He said: No. I said: Does he prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness? He said: No. I said: Do you supplicate sometimes in the obligatory before you give salam from the last tashahhud? He said: Yes. He said: The desire sometimes takes me in the obligatory, so I seek forgiveness and ask, with that little. He said: And they are not equal, supplication for the world and its aim is worse than supplication for the Hereafter and seeking forgiveness."
"3905","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysara, from Ata, and Mujahid, he said: When you are in the obligatory, do not supplicate with anything until the imam finishes. Ibrahim said: And I heard Tawus saying: Do not supplicate in the obligatory, and I know nothing after the two rak'as except the tashahhud."
"3906","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Supplicate in the obligatory with what is in the Quran. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, the same."
"3907","From al-Thawri, from Sadaqa ibn Yasar, from Tawus, he said: Supplicate in the obligatory with what is in the Quran. From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, like the saying of Tawus."
"3908","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: Supplicate in the obligatory with what you wish."
"3909","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, that Ibn Mas'ud used to say: Carry your needs upon the obligatory. And Amr ibn Dinar and others of our scholars said: There is no prayer more beloved to me to supplicate in it my need than the obligatory. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: And I looked at the opening of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his companions, the obligatory, I find them supplicating and seeking forgiveness in some of their bowing and prostration, so there is no harm in that."
"3910","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hafs ibn al-Furafisa, he said: One narrating told me, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, that he used to supplicate for al-Zubayr and Asma his mother, naming them in prayer by their names."
"3911","From Ma'mar ibn Rashid, and another, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Hafs ibn al-Furafisa, that he heard Urwa ibn al-Zubayr saying in his prayer while prostrating: O Allah, forgive al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, and Asma bint Abi Bakr."
"3912","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Do you dislike seeking forgiveness in the voluntary? He said: Yes, until he sits and recites tashahhud, then seeks forgiveness sitting. He said: And establish prayer for My remembrance."
"3913","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It reached me that the Muslims used to speak in prayer as the Jews and Christians speak, until it was revealed: And when the Quran is recited, listen to it and be silent."
"3914","From al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, he said: I prayed beside Abd Allah, and I did not know what he recites until I heard him saying: Increase me in knowledge, so I knew he is in Ta-Ha."
"3915","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn Ubayda, from Sila ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfa, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he passed a verse of fear would seek refuge, and when he passed a verse of mercy would ask."
"3916","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, that both of them saw no harm in a man supplicating in voluntary prayer, when he passes a verse mentioning Paradise and Hell, pausing at it to ask and seek refuge."
"3917","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, that Aishah came upon this verse: So Allah favored us and saved us from the torment of the scorching wind, and she said: My Lord, favor me and save me from the torment of the scorching wind."
"3918","From al-Thawri, from al-Suddi, from Abd Khayr al-Hamdani, he said: I heard Ali recite in prayer: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and he said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."
"3919","From al-Thawri, from Mis'ar, from Umayr ibn Said, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari recited on Friday: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High, and: Has the story of the Overwhelming reached you."
"3920","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that when he recited: Is He not able to give life to the dead, he would say: Glory be to You, O Allah, yes indeed, and when he recited: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, he said: Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."
"3921","From Ma'mar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he recited al-Tin and reached: Is not Allah the most just of judges, would say: Yes indeed, and when he recited: Is He not able to give life to the dead, would say: Yes indeed, and when he recited: In what message after it will they believe, about what was revealed, or he said: We believe in Allah and what He sent down."
"3922","From Ma'mar, from Shaddad ibn Jaban, he said: I spent the night with Hujr al-Madari, and I heard him praying at night; he recited and came upon this verse: Do you see what you emit, is it you who create it or are We the Creator, and he said: Nay, it is You, O Lord, nay, it is You, O Lord, nay, it is You, O Lord, three times; then he recited: Do you see what you till, is it you who grow it or are We the Grower, and he said: Nay, it is You, O Lord, nay, it is You, O Lord, nay, it is You, O Lord, three times; then he recited: Do you see the water you drink, is it you who sent it down from the clouds or are We the Sender, and he said: Nay, it is You, O Lord, nay, it is You, O Lord, three times; then he said: Do you see the fire you kindle, is it you who produced its tree or are We the Producer, and he said: Nay, it is You, O Lord, saying it three times."
"3923","From Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, he disliked that a man pass by mention of the Fire and seek refuge from it in the obligatory or voluntary prayer; he said: And al-Hasan saw no harm in it in voluntary prayer."
"3924","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: It was disliked that when the imam passes a verse of warning or a verse of mercy, those behind him say anything."
"3925","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim, from Mujahid, about: And when the Quran is recited, listen to it and be silent, he said: This is about prayer."
"3926","From Mansur, from Malik ibn al-Harith, he said: Allah, Exalted be His praise, says: When the servant is occupied with My Book instead of asking Me, I give him better than what I give to those who ask."
"3927","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: Is supplication in voluntary prayer like that in the prescribed prayer, if I name a person does it break my prayer? He said: Yes; if you said it and your prayer is odd, add a rakah to make it even, then leave and resume your prayer."
"3928","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked: Does a man pray with his mouth covered? He said: I prefer that you remove it from your mouth; I heard Abu Hurayrah say: When you pray, you are conversing privately with your Lord."
"3929","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he disliked that a man place his hand or his garment over his mouth or over his nose in prayer."
"3930","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he disliked that a man pray while veiled."
"3931","From Abdullah ibn Umar and Ibn Abi Rawwad, or one of them, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to dislike that a man pray while veiled."
"3932","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he disliked that a man pray while veiled; and he used to say: When a man sneezes in prayer, let him praise Allah without raising his voice; and I asked him about a man who sneezes in the privy, and he said: He praises Allah, for it ascends."
"3933","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: Jadah ibn Hubayrah saw a man with a helmet praying, so he sent a man to him saying: Uncover the helmet from your mouth."
"3934","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that al-Hasan used to permit a man to pray while veiled if it was due to cold or an excuse."
"3935","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Should I say in the prescribed prayer: Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah, gesturing with my hand, then straighten into the row? He said: Yes, that is fine."
"3936","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women, iss iss in prayer; Ata said: And Abu Hurayrah uttered iss iss in prayer; he said: Abu Hurayrah said in prayer: Likewise among the speech of men and women; and Ata preferred women to glorify Allah rather than clap, rather than iss iss; Ata said: And Abu Hurayrah clapped with his hands."
"3937","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women in prayer."
"3938","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women in prayer."
"3939","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women."
"3940","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women regarding permission to enter."
"3941","Ma'mar told us, from Abu Hazim, he said: I was with Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi when it was said to him: There was something between Banu Amr ibn Awf and the people of Quba; he said: That was long ago; we were in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when word came to him that there was something among the people of Quba, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to them to make peace between them, and he was delayed from the people; Bilal said to Abu Bakr: Shall I call the prayer? He said: As you wish; so Bilal called it and the people put Abu Bakr forward; while he was praying, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, making his way through the rows until he stood behind Abu Bakr, and they began to clap, though he would not turn in prayer; when they increased, he turned, and there was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing behind him; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him to pray as he was, but he stepped back, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced and prayed; when he finished, he said: What prevented you, when I commanded you, from continuing to pray? He said: It is not fitting for the son of Abu Quhafah to lead the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is this clapping in prayer? Tasbih is for men and clapping is for women."
"3942","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out one day to the mosque and said: Where is the young man of Daws? He said: There he is, O Messenger of Allah, ill in the rear of the mosque; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me, wiped over my head, and said something kind to me; then he turned to the people and said: If I forget in my prayer, let the men glorify Allah and let the women clap; he said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed and did not forget anything in his prayer; and with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were two and a half rows of men and two rows of women, or two rows of men and two and a half rows of women."
"3943","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill and ordered Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed for the people sitting, placing Abu Bakr behind him between him and the people; he said: And the people prayed behind him standing; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I known beforehand what I know now, you would only have prayed sitting following the prayer of your imam; if he prays standing, pray standing, and if he prays sitting, pray sitting."
"3944","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed and ordered Abu Bakr, who stood beside him at his side; he recited, and when he finished and it was the rakah, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and bowed and prostrated with the people; I said: How much did he pray, and which prayer was that? He said: I do not know, except that it was a prayer with recitation."
"3945","From Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out one day while Abu Bakr was leading the people in prayer; Abu Bakr started to step back, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him to pray as he was; he said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and sat beside him; the people prayed following the prayer of Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr prayed following the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting."
"3946","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came during his illness until he sat in his place of prayer, and Abu Bakr stood beside him, praying standing while following the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people followed Abu Bakr."
"3947","Ma'mar told us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell from a horse and injured his right side; they visited him and he led them sitting, gesturing to them to sit; when he gave the salutation, he said: The imam is appointed only to be followed, so when he says the takbir, say the takbir, and when he bows, bow, and when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You belongs praise, and when he prostrates, prostrate, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting all together; Abu Urwah said: And it has reached me that this is not fitting for anyone besides the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3948","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell from a horse and injured his right side; he led them sitting and they prayed with him sitting; when he finished, he said: The imam is appointed only to be followed, so when he prays standing, pray standing, and when he bows, bow, and when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: Our Lord, to You belongs praise, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting all together."
"3949","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed sitting, leading the people, while the people stood behind him; he gestured with his hand behind him, signaling to them to sit."
"3950","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Amr ibn Ubayd told me, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill, and Umar and a group with him visited him; prayer came and he led them sitting while they stood, gesturing to them with his hand to sit; when he finished, he said: The Persians only honored their kings because they sit while others stand for them, so do not do that; and he gestured with his hand behind him without raising them to his shoulders."
"3951","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam is appointed only to be followed, so do not differ from him; when he says the takbir, say the takbir, and when he bows, bow, and when he says: Allah hears whoever praises Him, say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise, and when he prostrates, prostrate, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting all together."
"3952","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The imam is a leader, so if he prays sitting, pray sitting, and if he prays standing, pray standing."
"3953","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Qays ibn Qahd al-Ansari told me that their imam fell ill in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: He used to lead us sitting while we were sitting."
"3954","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that Usayd ibn Hudayr fell ill and used to lead his people sitting."
"3955","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I prefer that when the imam falls ill, he appoint someone to lead the people if he can only pray sitting; he said: And if the imam prays sitting, that is the Sunnah; I said: If he prays sitting, shall I pray with him or leave him? He said: Rather pray with him; do you turn away from the Sunnah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: And I prefer that they put forward another of them."
"3956","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no man be followed as imam while sitting after me."
"3957","From Israil, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no man be followed as imam while sitting after me; he said: And I have only seen the people following the imam: when he prays sitting, those behind him pray sitting, and it is a Sunnah from more than one."
"3958","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah al-Sahmi, from Hafsah, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray sitting until a year or two before his death; he would pray his supererogatory prayer sitting, reciting the surah slowly until it was longer in recitation than another."
"3959","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman told me that Abu Salamah told him that Aishah told him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until most of his prayer was sitting."
"3960","Al-Thawri told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salamah, she said: By Him Who took his soul, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he did not die until much of his prayer was sitting except the prescribed prayer; and the deed most beloved to him was what its doer persists in, even if little."
"3961","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: I heard the family of Aishah mention that she used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exerted his body strenuously in worship, except that when he advanced in age and grew heavy, most of his prayer was sitting."
"3962","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: It has reached us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until he prayed sitting."
"3963","Ma'mar told us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray sitting, and when near his bowing he would stand and recite thirty or forty verses, then bow."
"3964","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not recite any of the night prayer sitting until he advanced in age; when thirty or forty verses remained, he would stand and recite them, then prostrate."
"3965","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: We asked Aishah about the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed standing, he bowed standing, and when he prayed sitting, he bowed sitting."
"3966","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: We asked Aishah about the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray a long night standing and a long night sitting; I said: How would he do? She said: When he recited standing, he bowed standing, and when he recited sitting, he bowed sitting."
"3967","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It is recommended for a man, when he intends to pray sitting, to begin his prayer with two rakahs standing."
"3968","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: A man may pray sitting in voluntary prayer if he wishes, cross-legged or in ihtiba; he said: And stretch your leg if you wish after the tashahhud; he said: I said: Reclining? He said: No."
"3969","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he would sit in ihtiba at the end of his prayer in voluntary prayer."
"3970","Al-Thawri mentioned, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: When he wants to prostrate, he folds his leg and prostrates."
"3971","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: When a man wants to pray sitting cross-legged, then when he wants to bow he draws his thigh as one sits in prayer, then bows and prostrates; and the view of Ibn al-Musayyab is dearer to Sufyan."
"3972","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, that he used to pray sitting cross-legged."
"3973","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Muhammad, he said: A man prays sitting cross-legged."
"3974","From al-Thawri, from a shaykh of the Ansar, he said: I saw Anas praying cross-legged."
"3975","From al-Thawri, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Haytham ibn Shihab, he said: Abdullah said: For a man to sit on heated stones is better than sitting cross-legged in prayer; Abd al-Razzaq said: That means, when he prayed standing, he should not sit for the tashahhud cross-legged; but if he prayed sitting, let him sit cross-legged."
"3976","From Abdullah, from Shubah, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to dislike sitting cross-legged in prayer, meaning voluntary prayer; Shubah said: So I asked Hammad about it, and he said: There is no harm in it in voluntary prayer."
"3977","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Amr, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr, that he used to pray in ihtiba until ten verses remained, then he would stand and recite, then bow."
"3978","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Ata prayed in ihtiba, and Said ibn Jubayr passed by him and said: As if you are sitting chatting; then he loosened his ihtiba, and when he left, Ata released the ihtiba while praying."
"3979","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I saw Ibn Sirin praying sitting cross-legged."
"3980","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah told me that Uthman ibn Muhammad told him that Muzahim, the freedman of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, said to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: I marvel at a man praying proudly in ihtiba; it is nothing; Umar replied to him and said: It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until most of his prayer was sitting."
"3981","From Ma'mar, that Ata al-Khurasani used to sit in ihtiba in voluntary prayer, and I said to him: From whom did you take this? And I told him the hadith of al-Zuhri from Ibn al-Musayyab; he said: I think I took it only from Ibn al-Musayyab."
"3982","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Abu al-Zinad, he said: I saw Ibn al-Musayyab praying in ihtiba in voluntary prayer."
"3983","From Ma'mar, or another, that Ibn Sirin used to pray in ihtiba in voluntary prayer."
"3984","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: May I begin the prayer standing, then pray reciting sitting, without bowing or prostrating? He said: Yes; I said: May I bow one rakah, then sit and recite? He said: No, I dislike that you sit in an odd rakah; I said: May I begin, then sit without bowing or prostration? He said: Yes, if you wish; you are not now in an odd rakah; I said: What if I sat after one rakah? He said: Prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, but sit in even rakahs as you wish."
"3985","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I began the prayer standing, bowed a rakah and prostrated, then stood; may I sit if I wish without bowing or prostration? He said: No."
"3986","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me that he heard Alqamah ibn Nadlah report that he saw Ibn Umar: while a man was praying in ihtiba with his knees drawn together, joining his hands together between his knees, Ibn Umar pulled him, then gestured to him to place his palms upon his knees."
"3987","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Abdullah ibn Umar said: We arrived at Medina and were afflicted by a severe fever of Medina; the people often prayed their supererogatory prayer sitting; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them at midday while they prayed their supererogatory prayer sitting, and said: The prayer of one sitting is half the prayer of one standing; he said: And the people then strained to stand."
"3988","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: Ibn Shihab said: Anas ibn Malik told me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Medina while it was feverish, and the people fell ill; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while the people prayed sitting, and said: The prayer of one sitting is half the prayer of one standing; so the people strained to pray standing."
"3989","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: One sitting in prayer has half the reward of one standing."
"3990","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Yahya, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he prayed sitting, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was told that you said: The prayer of one sitting is half the prayer of one standing, yet you pray sitting? He said: Yes, but I am not like any of you."
"3991","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: May I pray sitting if I wish, without illness? He said: Yes, if you wish, but the prayer of one sitting is half the reward of one standing."
"3992","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Yusuf ibn Mahak told me, from one of their women, that she visited Aishah and prayed Asr, then stood and prayed two rakahs after it; he said: Then she visited Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and prayed Asr, then stood and prayed rakahs after it sitting; the woman said: O Umm Salamah, I visited your sister Aishah and she prayed two rakahs after Asr; Umm Salamah said: Aishah is younger than me, and I am old."
"3993","Abu Said said: Perhaps from al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Maymun ibn Mihran, from his father, he said: It was said to him: What is the sign for a sick person praying sitting? He said: When he cannot stand for his worldly affairs, let him pray sitting."
"3994","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim how the sick person prays; he said: His standing posture is cross-legged; from al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, the same."
"3995","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Haytham, he said: I visited Ibrahim while he was ill, praying reclining on his right side, gesturing for the Zuhr prayer; he said: And others among the jurists used to say: Lying on his back, with his feet toward the qiblah, as if standing he would face the qiblah."
"3996","From Abu Bakr ibn Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Ubaydullah his father, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar said: The sick person prays lying on his back with his feet toward the qiblah."
"3997","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When the sick person can only pray reclining, he prays on his side facing the qiblah, gesturing."
"3998","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: A sick person lying down unable to sit; he said: Let him pray inclined, and if he cannot, let him pray lying down, gesturing with his head; he said: I said: Should he place his hands on his knees when he bows and prostrates? He said: No, but let him gesture with his head and his hands, and raise his hands for the takbir."
"3999","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the sick person prays sitting, then when he bows he places his hands on his knees, and when he prostrates he places his hands on the ground if he can."
"4000","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When the sick person bows, he places his hands on his knees, and when he prostrates, he places his hands on the ground."
"4001","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard Qatadah asked about a sick person with an abscess or the like, how does he pray? He said: In every posture, lying down or inclined; if he faces the qiblah and can only do that, he gestures, making his prostration lower than his bowing."
"4002","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If he cannot prostrate on the ground, should he prostrate on a mat, or should pebbles be raised to him on a palm-leaf mat to prostrate on? He said: No, but let him gesture with his head, making the prostration lower than the bowing."
"4003","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Ibn Umar visited Safwan al-Tawil while he prayed on a cushion, and forbade him to pray on pebbles or on a cushion, ordering him to gesture."
"4004","Sulayman ibn Musa said: Nafi told us that Ibn Umar used to say: When one of you is sick and cannot prostrate on the ground, let him not raise anything to his face; let him make his prostration like his bowing, gesturing with his head; and Nafi saw that Ibn Umar, in the illness in which he died, prayed placing his forehead once, then could not after that, making his prostration like his bowing."
"4005","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, he said: Ibn Umar visited Ibn Safwan al-Tawil and found him prostrating on a cushion; he forbade him and said: Gesture, making the prostration lower than the bowing."
"4006","From al-Thawri, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, he said: I heard Ibn Umar asked by a man on a wooden structure while he was sick, and he said: I do not command you to take idols besides Allah; whoever can pray standing, let him pray standing, and if he cannot, sitting, and if he cannot, reclining while gesturing."
"4007","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Ibn Umar used to say: When the sick person cannot prostrate on the ground, he gestures; and Qatadah used to dislike for the sick person to prostrate on a wall or to raise pebbles or anything to his face."
"4008","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: When the sick person cannot bow or prostrate, he gestures with his head in bowing and prostration while saying the takbir."
"4009","From Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: When the sick person cannot bow, he gestures with his head."
"4010","From Ismail ibn Abdullah, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, he said: My father was stricken with paralysis, so he sent me to Ibn Umar: Should something be raised to him when he prays? Ibn Umar said: No, rather gesture with what is between your eyes."
"4011","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Muawiyah, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, that Ibn Masud visited his brother Utbah while he prayed toward a siwak raised to his face; he took it and threw it, then said: Gesture, and let your bowing be higher than your prostration."
"4012","Ma'mar told us, from Qatadah, from Umm al-Hasan, she said: I saw Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), prostrate on a cushion while sitting, meaning praying sitting."
"4013","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Fazarah al-Sulami, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about a sick person prostrating on a pure cushion, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"4014","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The sick person prostrates on a pure cushion and on a pure garment."
"4015","Al-Thawri told us, from Qabus ibn Abi Zabyan, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no harm in the sick person folding a garment and prostrating on it."
"4016","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah told me that Urwah used to pray on something raised above the ground."
"4017","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he used to permit the sick person to pray on his mount facing the qiblah."
"4018","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: There is no harm in the sick person praying on his mount, facing the House, not turning away from it."
"4019","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar lost consciousness for a day and a night, and did not make up what he missed."
"4020","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar lost consciousness for a month and did not make up what he missed, praying the day in which he recovered."
"4021","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When the sick person loses consciousness then regains his mind, he does not repeat the prayer; Ma'mar said: I asked al-Zuhri about that, and he said: He does not make up."
"4022","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: He makes up the prayer of his day and the prayer of his night if he was unconscious."
"4023","From al-Thawri, from al-Suddi, he said: Yazid told me that Ammar ibn Yasir was wounded and lost consciousness during Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha, then recovered at midnight and prayed Zuhr, then Asr, then Maghrib, then Isha."
"4024","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When the sick person is overpowered in his mind then recovers, let him pray what he missed, when he regains his prayer, each day and night likewise."
"4025","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar once fell ill, was overpowered in his mind until he left prayer, then recovered, and did not pray what he had left of prayer."
"4026","From al-Thawri, about a man who lost consciousness and missed one prayer, not knowing which it was, he said: He begins by praying Maghrib, then Fajr, then Zuhr, intending thereby Zuhr, Asr, and Isha; whichever it was, that is four."
"4027","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: About the imbecile who regains sanity at times, he said: He does not make up prayer when he regains his mind."
"4028","From al-Thawri: Does the sleeper and the drunkard make up prayer, while the sick does not?"
"4029","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, about the madman who recovers, he said: He performs ablution."
"4030","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: He takes a bath."
"4031","Ma'mar told us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about the imbecile who regains sanity at times, he said: He does not make up prayer when he regains his mind."
"4032","Ibn Jurayj told us, he said: I said to Ata: Reciting to music? He said: There is no harm in that; I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: Dawud the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take the stringed instrument and play it over it, repeating his voice over it, seeking to weep thereby and to make others weep."
"4033","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has never listened to a prophet as He has listened to a prophet chanting the Quran."
"4034","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab told me, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has never listened to a prophet as He has listened to a prophet chanting the Quran; a companion of his said: He added in it: reciting aloud."
"4035","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that he heard Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman report, I think from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah has never listened to anything as He has listened to a man with beautiful modulation of the Quran, meaning listened, he says: He hears."
"4036","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salamah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has never listened to a prophet as He has listened to a man with beautiful modulation of the Quran."
"4037","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar al-Qari and al-Mutawakkil ibn Abi Nuhayk visited Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, and Sad said to Abdullah: Who is this? He said: Al-Mutawakkil ibn Abi Nuhayk; he said: Yes, traders and earners, traders and earners, they delay; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran."
"4038","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Nuhayk, from Sad ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who does not chant the Quran."
"4039","From Ma'mar, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah listens to a man of beautiful voice, I think he meant chanting the Quran."
"4040","From Abdullah ibn al-Muharrar, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has an ornament, and the ornament of the Quran is a beautiful voice."
"4041","From Ma'mar, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz had a beautiful voice; he went out one night praying in the mosque, raising his voice, and the people gathered; Said ibn al-Musayyib sent word to him: You have tempted the people, so he did not repeat it."
"4042","Al-Thawri told us, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Talhah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah al-Nahmi, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah and His angels bless the first rows; beautify the Quran with your voices; whoever grants a milch animal, or a gift of silver, or shows the way to a lane, it equals freeing a neck."
"4043","Ma'mar told us, from Mansur, from Talhah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara ibn Azib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beautify your voices with the Quran; then he mentioned like the hadith of al-Thawri."
"4044","From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the voice of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari reciting, and said: Abu Musa has been given of the flutes of the family of Dawud."
"4045","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Malik ibn Mighwal, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Buraydah report from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the voice of al-Ashari Abu Musa reciting, and said: This one has been given a flute of the flutes of the family of Dawud; so he told him of that, and he said: Now you are my true friend, since you told me this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: Had I known that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) listened to my recitation, I would have beautified it greatly; he said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard another voice and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does he say it showing off? And I did not answer the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he repeated it to me once, twice, or three times; then I said after two or three: Does he say it showing off? Nay, he is penitent; he said: And he heard another supplicating: O Allah, I ask You by my bearing witness that You are Allah besides Whom there is no god, the One, the Eternal, Who did not beget nor was begotten, and none is equal to You; and he said: He has asked Allah by His name by which, when invoked, He answers, and when asked, He gives."
"4046","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: When Abu Musa sat with Umar ibn al-Khattab, he would sometimes say to him: Remind us of our Lord, O Abu Musa; and he would recite."
"4047","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar used to say to Abu Musa, while sitting with him in a gathering: Remind us, O Abu Musa; and he would recite."
"4048","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umar used to say to Abu Musa, while sitting with him in the gathering: Remind us of our Lord; and he would recite before him."
"4049","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: While Usayd ibn Hudayr al-Ansari was praying one night, Usayd said: Something like a cloud with things like lamps covered me, while the woman slept beside me and she was pregnant, and the horse was tied in the house; he said: I feared the horse would shy and the woman would panic and miscarry; so I left my prayer, and mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning, and he said to me: Recite, O Usayd; that was an angel listening to the Quran."
"4050","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Usayd ibn Hudayr said: While I, O Messenger of Allah, was reciting last night on my rooftop, something like a cloud covered me, while my wife was pregnant and the horse was tied; I feared my wife would give birth and my horse would shy; and he said: Recite, O Usayd, for it is the angel listening to the Quran; saying it three times."
"4051","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah al-Nakhai, from Sad ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib urged the people to use the siwak, saying: When a man stands praying, the angel draws near listening to the Quran, and keeps drawing near until he places his mouth upon his mouth; no verse he utters but falls into the belly of the angel; he said: So keep clean what is there, and Ali loved the siwak."
"4052","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim told me, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Who recites best? He said: The one whose recitation makes you see that he fears Allah; and by Allah, I never heard a recitation better than the recitation of Habib, Tawus the speaker."
"4053","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one told me, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard a man, they mentioned he was al-Hakam al-Ghifari, say: O plague, take me tonight; Abu Hurayrah said: Did you not hear, O father of so-and-so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Then none of you should pray for death, for he does not know upon what state of it he is; he said: Yes, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention six I fear may overtake me; he said: The selling of judgment, the shedding of blood, the rule of fools, the multitude of police, the severing of kinship, and people who take the Quran as flutes, chanting with it."
"4054","From Ma'mar, from Abu Jamrah al-Dubai, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: I am hasty in my speech and recitation; Ibn Abbas said: For me to recite al-Baqarah slowly is dearer to me than to race through the whole Quran."
"4055","From Ma'mar, he said: A man asked Mujahid: A man recited al-Baqarah and Al Imran in one rakah, their standing one, their bowing and prostration one, and their sitting one, which is better? He said: The one who recited al-Baqarah; he said: Then Mujahid recited: And a Quran We divided that you may recite it to people at intervals; he said: Slowly."
"4056","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What is His saying: And We recited it in slow recitation? He gestured with his hand: It is throwing, it is discarding, as if he dislikes slow recitation; he said: I think he means thereby energizing the man."
"4057","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: And We recited it in slow recitation means: part following part."
"4058","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: And Mujahid said: Slowly, slowly."
"4059","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, about slow recitation, he said: Recite it until you understand it."
"4060","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I utter the Quran in the prescribed and voluntary prayer, without repeating anything of it, hastening? He said: That suffices you."
"4061","Ma'mar reported from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sa'd ibn Hisham, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one skilled in the Quran is with the noble righteous scribes, and the one who recites while it is hard upon him has two rewards."
"4062","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if I repeat something of it? He said: I dislike that in prayer, so do not repeat any of it in voluntary or obligatory prayer. He said: I said: What if I recite it before a man and I repeat? He said: That is only disliked in prayer."
"4063","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id ibn Ubayd, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr leading them in Ramadan repeating this verse: When the fetters are round their necks, O man, what has deceived you concerning your Generous Lord, Who created you and fashioned you, repeating it two or three times."
"4064","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata reported from Hakim ibn Iqal: That he used to forbid raising the voice with recitation by day in voluntary prayer. He said: And it is said: He may raise with it at night as much as he wishes."
"4065","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Nafi': Did Ibn Umar let you hear the recitation in voluntary prayer by day? He said: Yes, a little of the surah, and it was brief."
"4066","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari reported to me, from al-Hasan, he said: The daytime prayer is silent, the voice is not raised in it except the Friday prayer and the dawn prayer, and what is raised."
"4067","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: The daytime prayer is silent."
"4068","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: I heard Abu Ubaydah saying: The daytime prayer is silent."
"4069","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim, he said: Abu Ubaydah sent me to a man whom he heard reciting aloud by day, and he said: The daytime recitation is silent."
"4070","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, from Aswad ibn Hilal, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Whoever lets himself hear has not kept it low."
"4071","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever lets himself hear has not kept it low. He means: When he prays in what the recitation is said aloud in."
"4072","From Hisham, from Muhammad, he said: I asked Ubaydah, he said: I said: A man wishes to conceal his recitation. He said: Then let him make himself hear."
"4073","From Ma'mar, from Abu Umar al-Madani, he said: We asked Ibn Umar about the daytime recitation, so he stood praying and sometimes he let us hear a verse."
"4074","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abdullah ibn Hudhafah while he was praying, and he raised his voice, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do not make me hear, O Hudhafah, and make Allah the Exalted hear."
"4075","Ma'mar reported from Ata al-Khurasani, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from Aishah, he said: A man asked her: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raise his voice at night when he recited? She said: Sometimes he raised and sometimes he lowered. He said: Praise be to Allah Who made latitude in religion. He said: Did he pray witr at the beginning of the night? She said: Yes, sometimes he prayed witr at the beginning of the night and sometimes he prayed witr at its end. He said: Praise be to Allah Who made latitude in religion. He said: Did he sleep while junub? She said: Sometimes he washed before sleeping, and sometimes he slept before washing, but he performed ablution before sleeping. He said: Praise be to Allah Who made latitude in religion."
"4076","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abu Bakr while he was praying and keeping it low, and passed by Umar while he was reciting aloud, and passed by Bilal while he was mixing. In the morning they were all with him, and he said: I passed by you, O Abu Bakr, and saw you keeping it low. He said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you. He said: Raise a little. He said: I passed by you, O Umar, while you were reciting aloud. He said: May my father and mother be ransom, I make the Merciful hear and awaken the sleeper. He said: Lower, or he said: Lower a little. He said: And I passed by you, O Bilal, while you were mixing. He said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you, I mix the good with the good. He said: Recite each surah in its own manner."
"4077","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Harmalah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: I passed by you, O Abu Bakr, while you were keeping your recitation low. He said: I make Him Whom I confide in hear. He said: And I passed by you, O Umar, while you were reciting your recitation aloud. He said: I drive away Satan and awaken the drowsy. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Lower a little. He said: And I passed by you, O Bilal, while you were reciting this surah and part of that surah. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I mix the good with the good. He said: Recite the surah in its own manner."
"4078","From Ma'mar, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi reported to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm, he said: Amrah daughter of Abd al-Rahman spent the night with me, and I stood praying at night and kept my recitation low, so she said: Raise your voice, for Mu'adh the reciter and Aflah the freedman of Abu Ayyub used to wake us at night by raising their voices."
"4079","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: We asked Alqamah: How was the recitation of Abdullah at night? And he used to spend the night with him. He said: He used to let the household of Utbah, his brother, hear, and they were in a room before him."
"4080","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, that Abdullah used to let the people of the house hear his recitation at night."
"4081","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, he said: For you is the fill of your house, meaning in the night recitation. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from al-Hasan ibn Sa'd, from his father, the like of it."
"4082","Ma'mar reported from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in retreat in the mosque, and he heard them raising their voices with recitation while he was in a dome of his, so he uncovered the curtains and said: Verily each of you confides in his Lord, so let none of you harm another, and let none of you raise over another in recitation, or he said: In prayer."
"4083","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi reported to us, from Abu Hazim the freedman of the Ansar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a dome in the month of Ramadan, and a man was leading the supererogatory prayer, so he looked out at them with his head and said: What Allah wills. Then he said: The worshipper confides in his Lord, so when one of you prays let him consider what he confides to his Lord with, and let none of you recite aloud over another with the Quran."
"4084","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata reported to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) listened one night to Abu Bakr and he was keeping his recitation low in his prayer, and listened to Umar and he was raising his voice, and listened to Bilal and he was taking from this surah and from this surah, so he said: I listened to you, O Abu Bakr, and you were lowering your voice. He said: I lower it to confide in my Lord. He said: And I listened to you, O Umar, and you were raising your voice. He said: I repel Satan and awaken the sleeper. He said: And I listened to you, O Bilal, and you were taking from this surah and from this surah. He said: I join the good with the good, I mix part of it with part. He said: All of this is good."
"4085","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu Razin, he said: Abdullah said: Drowsiness in prayer is from Satan, and drowsiness in battle is security from Allah."
"4086","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir reported to us, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As al-Thaqafi, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Satan has come between me and my prayer and my recitation. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is a devil called Khinzab, so when you sense him, seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan and spit to your left three times."
"4087","From Ma'mar, from Hammam ibn Munabbih, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you rises at night and the Quran becomes difficult on his tongue and he does not know what he says, let him leave and lie down."
"4088","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you dozes while praying, let him sleep on his bed, for he does not know whether he supplicates against himself or for himself."
"4089","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abdullah said: Do not contend with this night, for you cannot bear it. When one of you dozes in his prayer, let him leave and sleep on his bed, for that is safer for him."
"4090","From al-Thawri, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Abu al-Darda or Abu Dharr, he said: There is no man who intends to rise for a portion of the night and his eyes overcome him for it, except Allah writes its reward for him, and his sleep is a charity with which Allah has been charitable to him. From Abu Ma'shar, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4091","Abd Allah ibn Umar reported from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: That he used to pray at night two by two, and by day four then saluting. Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi'. And al-Thawri from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar the like of it."
"4092","From Ma'mar, from Thabit, he said: I spent a night with Anas, and he prayed two by two, then saluted."
"4093","From Muqatil, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the night prayer, and he said: Two by two. I said: The daytime prayer? He said: Four."
"4094","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Amrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: In every two of the night and day there is a salutation."
"4095","Al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: By night and day the tashahhud suffices you in prayer, unless you have a need, then salute."
"4096","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, he said: The tashahhud suffices you, even if you pray a hundred rak'ahs."
"4097","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray on the day of the Confederates the noon, afternoon and night prayers until a part of the night had passed. He said: And that was before the fear prayer was revealed. So he commanded Bilal and he called the adhan, then he made the iqamah for the noon prayer, and he prayed it as he used to pray it in its time, then he commanded him and he made the iqamah for the afternoon prayer, and he prayed it as he used to pray it in its time, then he commanded him and he stood for the sunset prayer and prayed it in its time as he used to pray it in its time, then he commanded him and he stood for the night prayer and prayed it as he used to pray it in its time."
"4098","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the noon prayer with his Companions before the fear prayer was revealed. He said: The polytheists regretted that they had not attacked him. He said: A man said: They have a prayer before sunset which is dearer to them than themselves. They said: If they pray afterwards we shall attack them. So they watched for that, then the fear prayer was revealed, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer with them at the afternoon prayer."
"4099","From Ma'mar, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Mujahid, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer only twice, once at Dhu al-Riqa' in the land of Banu Sulaym, and once at Usfan while the polytheists were at Dajnan between them and the qiblah. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lined all his Companions behind him at Usfan, then advanced and prayed. He bowed with all of them, then prostrated with those next to him while the others stood behind him guarding him. When he prostrated two prostrations with them they stood, and those behind them prostrated. Then they advanced to the first rank and these stepped back. Then he bowed with all of them, then prostrated with those next to him while the others stood guarding them. When they raised their heads from prostration those prostrated. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saluted all of them, and their prayer was complete."
"4100","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said concerning His saying: If you fear that the disbelievers may persecute you: It was revealed on the day the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at Usfan and the polytheists at Dajnan, and they met. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the noon prayer with his Companions, four rak'ahs, their bowing, prostration and standing together at once, all of them. The polytheists intended to raid their baggage and fight them. So Allah the Exalted sent down upon him: Let a party stand. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the afternoon prayer, arranged his Companions in two ranks, magnified with all of them, and the first ones prostrated with his prostration while the others stood and did not prostrate, until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the first rank stood. Then he magnified with them and they all bowed, and the other rank advanced and the first rank stepped back, and they alternated prostration as they did the first time. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed the afternoon prayer as two rak'ahs."
"4101","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Usfan. He said: The polytheists faced us, over them Khalid ibn al-Walid, and they were between us and the qiblah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the noon prayer, and they said: They were in a state, had we seized their unguardedness. They said: There shall come upon them now a prayer dearer to them than their children and themselves. He said: Jibril came down with these verses between noon and afternoon: And when you are among them and establish the prayer for them. He said: The prayer came, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them and they took up arms. We lined behind him in two ranks. He said: Then he bowed and we all bowed. He said: Then he raised and we all raised. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated with the rank next to him. He said: And the others stood guarding them. When they prostrated and stood, the others sat and prostrated in their places. Then these advanced to the positions of these, and these came to the positions of these, and they all bowed. Then he raised and they all raised. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated with the rank next to him while the others stood guarding them. When they sat, the others sat and prostrated. Then he saluted them. Then he departed. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it twice, once at Usfan and once in the land of Banu Sulaym. From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with them the like of this prayer, except he did not mention the descent of Jibril. He said: And Jabir said: As your commanders do these days. From al-Thawri, from Hisham, the like of this from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4102","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Muslim, that Tawus informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the noon prayer, four rak'ahs, while he and the enemy were in one plain. The enemy said: They have another prayer dearer to them than the world and what is in it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying the afternoon prayer, and they stood behind him in two ranks. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed and the first rank bowed while the other rank stood. Then they stood and the first rank drew back, then stood in the station of the other rank, and the other advanced until they stood in their station. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed and the first rank bowed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two rak'ahs and each rank one rak'ah, then they prayed in their stations one rak'ah each."
"4103","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer with one of the two parties one rak'ah while the other party faced the enemy. Then they left and stood in the station of their companions facing the enemy, and those came and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one rak'ah with them. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saluted. Then these made up one rak'ah and these one rak'ah."
"4104","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, that Abd Allah ibn Umar used to report that he prayed it with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) magnified, and a party of us lined behind him while a party faced the enemy. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed one rak'ah for them with two prostrations, prostrating like half the dawn prayer. Then they left and faced the enemy, and the other party came and lined with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they did that. Then he saluted. Then every man of the two parties stood praying for himself one rak'ah with two prostrations."
"4105","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I heard him reporting from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the fear prayer. He said: Every man of the people prayed one rak'ah with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then every man prayed one rak'ah for himself."
"4106","From Isra'il and others, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: A party advances with the imam and a party opposite the enemy. The imam prays with them one rak'ah with two prostrations. Then the party that prayed with the imam goes and stands in the station of their companions, and those come and enter the imam's prayer, and he prays with them one rak'ah. Then the imam salutes. Then they stand and pray one rak'ah in their place. Then they go and stand in the station of their companions, and those come and pray one rak'ah."
"4107","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he lined a rank behind him and a rank facing the enemy. He said: And all of them were in prayer. He said: He magnified and they all magnified. He prayed with the rank next to him one rak'ah while a rank faced the enemy. Then these went and these came, and he prayed with them one rak'ah. Then these who prayed the second rak'ah with him stood and lined in their place. Then these went to the stations of these, and these came to these, and they made up one rak'ah."
"4108","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: A rank stands behind the imam and a rank facing the enemy, not in prayer. He said: The imam prays with those with him one rak'ah. Then these go to the stations of these, and these come, and he prays with them one rak'ah. Then the imam salutes. Then these return to the stations of these, and these return and line up one rak'ah. Then these leave to the stations of these, and these return and make up one rak'ah. The imam has two rak'ahs, and each of the two groups one rak'ah with the imam and one rak'ah alone, except the first ones begin with the making up because they began the prayer, and they do not speak until they finish all their prayer, because they are in prayer."
"4109","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Salih ibn Khawwat ibn Jubayr, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, and he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The imam stands in the fear prayer, and a rank stands behind him and a rank facing the enemy. He said: He prays with these one rak'ah, and when he has prayed with them one rak'ah they stand in their place while the imam stands, and they make up one rak'ah. Then they go to the stations of those, and those come, and he prays with them one rak'ah. Then they stand in their place and make up one rak'ah."
"4110","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: One who witnessed Sa'id ibn al-As in a campaign called Dhat al-Khashab reported to me, and with him was Hudhayfah. Sa'id said: Which of you witnessed the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Hudhayfah said: I. Hudhayfah commanded them and they wore arms. Then he said: If any excitement startles you, fighting is lawful for you. He said: He prayed with one of the two parties one rak'ah while the other party faced the enemy. Then these left and stood in the station of those, and those came, and he prayed with them another rak'ah. Then he saluted them."
"4111","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, from Aswad ibn Hilal, from Tha'labah ibn Zahdam al-Hanzali, he said: We were with Sa'id ibn al-As, I think he said: At Tabaristan. He said: Which of you witnessed the fear prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Hudhayfah said: I. He said: A rank stood behind him and a rank facing the enemy. He said: He prayed with them the rak'ah. Then these went to the stations of these, and he prayed with them one rak'ah. Then he departed."
"4112","From al-Thawri, from al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi' ibn Umaylah al-Fazari, from al-Qasim ibn Hassan, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: I asked him about the fear prayer? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and prayed with them. A rank stood behind him and a rank facing the enemy, and he prayed with them one rak'ah. He said: Then these went to the stations of these, and these came, and he prayed with them one rak'ah. Then he departed."
"4113","From al-Thawri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Jahm, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the fear prayer at Dhi Qarad. He lined a rank behind him and a rank facing the enemy. He said: He prayed with the rank with him one rak'ah. Then these went to the stations of these, and he prayed with them one rak'ah. Then he saluted all of them. Then they departed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had two rak'ahs, and each of the two groups one rak'ah."
"4114","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: And how can it be shortened? Meaning when each of the two groups has one rak'ah."
"4115","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: And how can it be shortened? Meaning when each of the two groups has two rak'ahs, it is not shortening."
"4116","From al-Thawri, he said: The fear prayer. He said: The imam stands, and a rank stands behind him and a rank facing the enemy, not in prayer. He prays with the rank behind him one rak'ah. Then they withdraw on their heels facing the enemy, and the other rank comes and they pray with the imam one rak'ah. Then they stand and go to their stations while the imam sits, and the first ones come while the imam sits, and they bow and prostrate and do not recite, and sit with the imam. Then he stands with them and prays the second with them. Then the imam salutes. They go to their stations, and the others come and pray one rak'ah reciting in it, then sit and bear witness, then stand in their place and pray another rak'ah reciting in it only the Opening of the Book if they wish, and bear witness and salute."
"4117","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus reported to me that his father said: That you shorten the prayer if you fear the disbelievers may persecute you. He said: Its shortening is in fear and battle, prayer in every manner, riding and walking. He said: As for the two rak'ahs of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people's prayer in travel two rak'ahs, that is not shortening, it is its completion. Tawus says that."
"4118","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman said to Ata: A Muslim pursues the enemy on his trail and prays while pursuing him, turned away from the House. He said: Does he pray thus on his mount? He said: No, but when the Muslim is pursued and the enemy pursues him, let him perform it thus."
"4119","From Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: If the fear is more intense than that, as if he meant hand-to-hand fighting, they pray on foot, standing on their feet, or mounted, facing the qiblah or not facing it. He said: And I do not know Abd Allah except he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4120","Ibn Jurayj reported from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: If the fear is more intense than that, let them pray standing and mounted wherever their direction is."
"4121","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, he said: When the enemy overshadows them, it is lawful for them to pray toward whichever direction they are, on foot or mounted, two rak'ahs gesturing with gesture. Al-Zuhri mentioned it from Salim, from Ibn Umar."
"4122","From Sufyan, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim concerning His saying: But if you fear, then on foot or mounted. He said: Two rak'ahs, gesturing with his head with a gesture wherever his face is. Sufyan said: Riding or walking."
"4123","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: He gestures with one rak'ah."
"4124","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah concerning His saying: Then on foot or mounted. He said: That is at the striking with the sword. You pray one rak'ah with a gesture wherever your face is, whether riding or walking or hastening."
"4125","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Dahhak concerning His saying: But if you fear, then on foot or mounted. He said: Two magnifications suffice wherever his direction is."
"4126","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: When they mingle, it is only remembrance and signaling with the head."
"4127","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When there is sword-fighting, it is only one rak'ah with which he gestures, a gesture, wherever his face is, walking or riding."
"4128","Ma'mar reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: Two rak'ahs with which he gestures wherever his face is."
"4129","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab reported to me from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that Aishah the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him: That the prayer when first obligated was obligated as two rak'ahs. Then Allah completed the prayer in residence and the two rak'ahs were left as they were in travel. He said: I said to Urwah: What made Aishah pray four rak'ahs in travel though she knew Allah obligated it as two rak'ahs? Urwah said: She interpreted of that what Uthman interpreted in completing the prayer at Mina."
"4130","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina two rak'ahs, and with Abu Bakr two rak'ahs, and with Umar two rak'ahs, and with Uthman the beginning of his caliphate, then he prayed it four. Al-Zuhri said: It reached me that Uthman prayed it four only because he resolved to stay after the pilgrimage."
"4131","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman the beginning of his caliphate, used to pray at Makkah and at Mina two rak'ahs. Then Uthman prayed it four. That reached Ibn Mas'ud, and he said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. Then he stood and prayed four. It was said to him: You said the retrieval, then you prayed four? He said: Disagreement is evil."
"4132","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to travel from Madinah to Makkah fearing none but Allah and pray two rak'ahs. From Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas the like of it."
"4133","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said: In what was the shortening made in fear though the people are safe? He said: The Sunnah. I said: And a concession? He said: Yes."
"4134","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to shorten it therein as long as he stayed, meaning at Makkah in his travel, and Abu Bakr, and Umar, and Uthman until between the backs of his caliphate."
"4135","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: As for His saying: If you fear that the disbelievers may persecute you. He said: That is only when they fear the disbelievers. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) later established two rak'ahs by Sunnah, and it is not shortening but it is completion."
"4136","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Ammar reporting from Abd Allah ibn Babah, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, he said: I said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: Allah only said: That you shorten the prayer if you fear the disbelievers may persecute you, yet the people are safe. Umar said: I wondered at what you wondered at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: A charity with which Allah has been charitable to you, so accept His charity."
"4137","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umayyah ibn Abd Allah, that he said to Ibn Umar: We find the fear prayer and the residence prayer in the Quran, but we do not find the traveler's prayer. Ibn Umar said: Allah sent His Prophet while we were the most ignorant of people, so we do as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"4138","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Humayd al-Himyari asked Ibn Abbas, saying: I travel, do I shorten the prayer in travel or complete it? Ibn Abbas said: It is not its shortening but its completion, and the Sunnah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out safe fearing none but Allah and prayed two until he returned. Then Abu Bakr went out fearing none but Allah and prayed two rak'ahs until he returned. Then Umar went out safe fearing none but Allah and prayed two until he returned. Then Uthman did that two-thirds of his rule or half of it, then prayed it four. Then Banu Umayyah took it up. Ibn Jurayj said: It reached me that he completed four at Mina ever only because a bedouin called to him in the Khayf mosque at Mina: O Commander of the Faithful, I have continued praying them two rak'ahs since I saw you last year pray them two rak'ahs. Uthman feared the ignorant people would think the prayer is only two rak'ahs, and he completed it at Mina only ever for that."
"4139","From al-Thawri, from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Adha prayer is two rak'ahs, and the Fitr prayer is two rak'ahs, and the traveler's prayer is two rak'ahs, complete and not shortened, upon the tongue of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4140","From Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, he said: I met Ibn Umar and said: The prayer in travel. He said: Two rak'ahs. He said: I said: What do you think here at Mina? He said: Woe to you, have you heard of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I said: Yes, and I believe in Allah. He said: He used to pray two rak'ahs by two rak'ahs, so pray if you wish or leave."
"4141","From Isra'il, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, that Ali said: The traveler's prayer is two rak'ahs."
"4142","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Muwarraq al-Ijli, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about prayer in travel, and he said: Two rak'ahs by two rak'ahs. Whoever opposes the Sunnah has disbelieved."
"4143","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, they said: The traveler prays two rak'ahs until he returns, unless he enters one of the Muslim cities, for he completes."
"4144","From Isra'il, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Layla al-Kindi, he said: Sulayman came with twelve riders or thirteen of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the prayer came, they said: Advance, O Abu Abd Allah. He said: We do not lead you, nor marry your women. Allah has guided us through you. He said: A man of the people advanced and prayed four rak'ahs. When he saluted, Salman said: What have we to do with the fourfold! Half the fourfold would have sufficed us, and we are in greater need of the concession."
"4145","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Uthman wrote to one of his governors: That the resident does not pray the two rak'ahs, nor the nomad, nor the trader. Only one who has provisions and water-skin with him prays the two rak'ahs."
"4146","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, he said: One who read Uthman's letter, or it was read to him, informed me that Uthman wrote to the people of Basrah: As for what follows, it has reached me that some of you are in a pasture camp, or in trade, or a tax collector, and he shortens the prayer. Only one who is journeying or in presence of an enemy shortens the prayer."
"4147","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Ibn Mas'ud said: The prayer is not shortened except in pilgrimage or jihad."
"4148","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he said: Do not be deceived by your trades and your pasture camps, traveling to the farthest Sawad, saying: We are traveling people. The travelers are only from one horizon to another."
"4149","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim reported to me from Ibn Sa'id and Hudhayfah, that they used to say to the people of Kufah: Let not your pasture camp nor your Sawad deceive you. Do not shorten the prayer toward the Sawad. He said: And between them and the Sawad was thirty farsakhs."
"4150","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I do not think you should shorten the prayer except on a path of Allah's paths. And before that he did not say this saying; he used to say: He shortens in all that. He said: And Tawus, when a man asked him saying: I travel for some need of mine, do I shorten the prayer? He would be silent. And he said: When we went out as pilgrims or performers of umrah we prayed two rak'ahs."
"4151","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Their saying: Do not shorten the prayer except on the path of Allah. He said: I think that is so. I said: Why? He said: Because the imam of the God-fearing shortened the prayer only on a path of Allah's paths, pilgrimage, or umrah, or campaign, and which of the imams after him struck the earth seeking the world? I said: What about Ibn Abbas, did he go out for other than pilgrimage or umrah? He said: No, except his going out to Ta'if. I said: Jabir, Ibn Umar and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri? He said: None of them. I said: What do you think? He said: He said: I think you should shorten only on the path of Allah, on the path of good. And before that he did not say this saying; he used to shorten in all that."
"4152","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' reported to me that Ibn Umar used to shorten to property of his at Khaybar which he inspected, though now there is no pilgrimage, nor umrah, nor campaign."
"4153","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas went out to Ta'if shortening the prayer."
"4154","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that Ibn Umar bought something from a man, I think a she-camel, and went out to look at it and shortened the prayer. That was a journey of a full day, or four, thus, barids."
"4155","From Malik, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar went out to Khaybar and shortened the prayer."
"4156","From Malik, from Nafi', that he used to travel with Ibn Umar a barid and he did not shorten the prayer in it."
"4157","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, saying: Do I shorten the prayer to Arafah or to Mina? He said: No, but to Ta'if and to Juddah. Do not shorten the prayer except in a full day, and do not shorten in less than a day. If you go to Ta'if or to Juddah or the like of that of land, to land of yours or livestock, then shorten the prayer. When you arrive, complete."
"4158","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Dinar, from Ata, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas: Do I shorten the prayer to Arafah? He said: No. I said: To Mina? He said: No, but to Juddah, to Usfan and to Ta'if. If you arrive to family of yours or to livestock, complete the prayer."
"4159","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: A man asked Ibn Abbas, saying: Do I shorten the prayer to Mina? He said: No. He said: To Arafah? He said: No. He said: To Ta'if? He said: Yes."
"4160","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When you travel a day till evening, complete the prayer. If you add more, shorten."
"4161","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, he said: Salim informed me that Ibn Umar used to shorten the prayer in a journey of a full day."
"4162","Ma'mar said, and Ayyub reported to me from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to shorten the prayer in a journey of four, thus, barids."
"4163","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that Ibn Umar traveled to Rim and shortened the prayer, and it is a journey of thirty miles."
"4164","Malik said, and Nafi' reported to me, that Ibn Umar shortened the prayer to Dhat al-Nusub."
"4165","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' reported to me that Ibn Umar's shortest toward which he shortened the prayer was property of his which he inspected at Khaybar, and it is a journey of three qasids. He did not shorten in less than it. I said: How far is Khaybar? He said: Three qasids. I said: Ta'if? He said: Yes, from the easy road, and a little farther."
"4166","From Isra'il, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, he said: I heard Suwayd ibn Ghafalah saying: When you travel three, shorten the prayer."
"4167","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, he said: I asked Ibrahim and Sa'id ibn Jubayr: In how much do you shorten the prayer? They said: In a journey of three."
"4168","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, he said: When the journey is of two nights or more, shorten the prayer. And Qatadah holds by it."
"4169","From Isra'il, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la, he said: Suwayd ibn Ghafalah said to me: When you travel three, shorten the prayer."
"4170","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I was with Hudhayfah at al-Mada'in, and I sought permission to visit my family at Kufah. He permitted, and stipulated on me not to break fast nor pray two rak'ahs until I return to him."
"4171","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: He shortens the prayer in a journey of two days."
"4172","From Isra'il, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, he said: I asked Shaqiq ibn Salamah. I said: I go out to al-Mada'in or to Wasit. He said: Do not shorten the prayer."
"4173","From Isra'il, from Isa, he said: I saw Amir al-Sha'bi traveling to Wasit shortening the prayer and breaking fast."
"4174","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: One who takes the passes as a pilgrim or performer of umrah, and one who takes the easy road of the Ta'if route, does he shorten? He said: Yes."
"4175","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, he said: When you go out and spend the night away from your family, shorten. If you come to your family, complete."
"4176","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: The prayer is shortened to al-Mada'in, and it is twenty-seven farsakhs from Kufah."
"4177","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Madinah four, and I prayed with him the afternoon at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rak'ahs, and he had gone out traveling."
"4178","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah and Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Anas, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the noon at Madinah four, and the afternoon at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rak'ahs. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn al-Munkadir and Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Anas, the like of it."
"4179","From Hushaym, he said: Abu Harun reported to me from Abu Sa'id, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he traveled a farsakh would alight shortening the prayer."
"4180","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad al-Dili, that Ali when he went out to Basrah saw a reed hut, and said: Were it not for this reed hut we would have prayed two rak'ahs. I said: What is a reed hut? He said: A house of reeds."
"4181","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn al-Munkadir reported to me from Anas ibn Malik: That he prayed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Madinah the noon four, and I prayed with him at Dhu al-Hulayfah the afternoon two rak'ahs, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended Makkah."
"4182","From al-Thawri, from Wiqa ibn Iyas al-Asadi, he said: Ali ibn Rabi'ah al-Asadi reported to me, he said: We went out with Ali while we looked at Kufah, and he prayed two rak'ahs. Then he returned and prayed two rak'ahs while looking at the town. We said to him: Why do you not pray four? He said: Until we enter it."
"4183","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd al-Fayishi, he said: We went out with Ali to Siffin, and he prayed two rak'ahs between the bridge and the causeway."
"4184","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: That he used to shorten the prayer when he left the houses of Madinah, and shorten when he returned until he entered its houses."
"4185","From Malik, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar when he went out as a pilgrim or performer of umrah shortened the prayer at Dhu al-Hulayfah."
"4186","From Isra'il, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, he said: I went out with my father, and with Alqamah, al-Aswad and Amr ibn Maymun, and they shortened when they left the houses."
"4187","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: He used to shorten when he left the houses behind."
"4188","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When you intend travel and you pass the bridge or the trench, pray two rak'ahs."
"4189","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, he said: Alqamah used to shorten at al-Najaf, and al-Aswad used to shorten at al-Qadisiyyah when they intended Makkah."
"4190","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When a man goes out as a pilgrim and has not left the houses of the town when the prayer comes, if he wishes he shortens, and if he wishes he completes. I have not heard anything about that."
"4191","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, he said: When a man leaves his house heading on his way and has not left the town when the prayer comes, let him shorten it. Likewise when he enters the town returning from his travel, then the prayer comes, let him shorten it until he enters his house."
"4192","From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar when he left his house used to shorten the prayer until he returned to it."
"4193","From al-Thawri, he said: When he goes out at prayer time he prays two rak'ahs, just as if he enters the town at prayer time he prays four."
"4194","From al-Thawri, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, from his father, he said: When I stay in a land ten, I complete. If I say: I go out today or tomorrow, I pray two rak'ahs. If I stay a month, I pray two rak'ahs. From Ibrahim, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali, the like of it."
"4195","Abu Sa'id said: I found in another's book, from Ma'mar, and it is correct. He said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir reported to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed at Tabuk twenty days shortening the prayer."
"4196","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shortening the prayer until he came to Makkah, and he stayed there ten shortening until we returned."
"4197","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Asim, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed at Makkah seventeen nights shortening the prayer."
"4198","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah stayed at Khaybar forty nights shortening the prayer."
"4199","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar stayed at Adharbayjan six months shortening the prayer. He said: And he used to say: When you resolve residence, complete."
"4200","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Umar, he said: If I came to a land I would pray two rak'ahs as long as I did not resolve to stay, even if I stayed twelve nights. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"4201","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to say: When you resolve to stay twelve nights, complete the prayer."
"4202","From Umar ibn Dharr, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: Ibn Umar when he came to Makkah and intended to stay fifteen nights would send his mount ahead and complete the prayer."
"4203","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar wrote to Ibn Umar while he was in the land of Persia: We are staying till the crescent. He wrote: That I pray two rak'ahs. From al-Thawri, from Ibn Ajlan, from Nafi', the like of it."
"4204","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When you stay in a land four, pray four. From Malik, from Ata ibn Abd Allah al-Khurasani, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the like of it."
"4205","From al-Thawri, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind reported to me from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: When you resolve a stay of fifteen nights, complete."
"4206","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Harith informed me, he said: We arrived at Madinah, so I sent to Ibn al-Musayyib: we are staying some days in Madinah, shall we shorten? He said: Yes."
"4207","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar, from Sad, he said: We were with him in Sham for two months; we would pray full while he would shorten, so we spoke to him, and he said: We know better."
"4208","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Zakariyya ibn Umar narrated to me that Sad ibn Abi Waqqas came as a delegation to Muawiyah and stayed with him a month, shortening it, or the month of Ramadan, and he broke the fast in it."
"4209","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, he said: We were with him in one of the lands of Persia for two years, and he would not combine, nor pray more than two rakahs. From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, the like of it."
"4210","From Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Jafar ibn Abdillah, that Anas ibn Malik stayed in Sham for two months with Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, praying two rakahs, two rakahs."
"4211","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash and Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah: that he stayed in Khwarazm for two years and prayed two rakahs."
"4212","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, he said: We were with Masruq at al-Silsilah for years, while he was its governor, and he led us in prayer two rakahs, two rakahs until he left, seeking the Sunnah by that."
"4213","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Wail, that he went out with Masruq to al-Silsilah and he shortened, and stayed years shortening. He said: I said to him: O Abu Aishah, what prompts you to do this? He said: Seeking the Sunnah. And he shortened until he returned."
"4214","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: We stayed with a governor, he said: I think in Sijistan, for two years, and with us were men from the companions of Ibn Masud, and he led us in prayer two rakahs, two rakahs until he left, then he said: That is how Ibn Masud used to do."
"4215","From Yasin, from Abu Ishaq, from Zaidah ibn Umayr, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: I go out traveling and stay years settled and resident, shall I shorten? He said: It is not shortening but completion, so pray two rakahs, two rakahs."
"4216","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: He prays two rakahs, even if he stays a year."
"4217","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Asma ibn Ubayd, he said: I asked al-Shabi at the time of Hajj? He said: I said: I come to Kufah, and in it are my grandmother and my family? He said: So he said: Which of the cities is most excellent, or he said: greatest? Then he answered me, saying: Is it not Madinah? I said: Yes. He said: I asked Ibn Umar about that, and he said: I come to the house in which I was born, meaning Makkah, and I do not pray more than two rakahs. Al-Shabi said: So I would stay a year or two years praying two rakahs, or he said: I do not add to two rakahs, two rakahs. From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Asma, from al-Shabi, the like of it."
"4218","From Israil, from Isa ibn Abi Azzah, he said: Amir al-Shabi stayed with us at al-Nahrayn for four months, not praying more than two rakahs."
"4219","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Yazid al-Rishk, he said: Abu Mijlaz narrated to us, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar when a man came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah? He said: That you set up another god with Allah. He said again: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah? He said: That you take rivals besides Allah. He said again: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what is associating partners with Allah? He said: I adjure you, if you are a Muslim, to leave me. So the man left, and Ibn Umar became intensely angry. He said: So I stood up, when I saw the severity of his anger, to leave, but he struck my knee with his hand and said: Sit, for I hope you are not of them. He said: I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I come to Madinah seeking something, and stay there seven months and eight months, how should I pray? He said: Pray two rakahs, two rakahs."
"4220","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdillah, he said: How did Ibn Umar do? He said: When midday had passed and he said: We are staying, he would complete the prayer; and he said: When he would say: today and tomorrow, he would shorten the prayer, even if he stayed twenty nights."
"4221","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: As for what I would be preparing in a land, saying: I am leaving now, now, then I shorten the prayer; but if I resolve to stay, then I pray full. I said: I am staying ten days? He said: Then pray full."
"4222","From Mamar, from a man, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: When you set down your saddle in a land, then complete the prayer."
"4223","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was said to Ata: A man traveling in the sand for nearly a month, seeking pasture each day, does he shorten? He said: No; people moving about their properties, staying in between that."
"4224","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar led the people of Makkah in the Zuhr prayer, then gave salam after two rakahs, then said: Complete your prayer, O people of Makkah, for we are travelers."
"4225","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar led the people of Makkah in the Zuhr or the Asr prayer, then gave salam after two rakahs, then said: Complete your prayer, O people of Makkah, for we are travelers."
"4226","From al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, he said: Umar led the people of Makkah in two rakahs, then said: O people of Makkah, we are travelers, so complete the prayer."
"4227","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn Umar visited a man of the people of Makkah, then the prayer came, so Ibn Umar led them two rakahs, then turned to them and said: Complete."
"4228","From Malik, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Safwan ibn Abdillah, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar came to visit Abdullah ibn Safwan, and he prayed for us two rakahs then left, so we stood and completed."
"4229","From al-Thawri concerning a traveler who prayed these two, then his ablution broke and he put forward a traveler who led them in four: He said: They repeat."
"4230","From al-Thawri concerning a traveler who leads resident people and prays four with them: He said: It does not suffice them; they start anew, though he has shortened his own prayer."
"4231","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hasan concerning a traveler who forgets and prays Zuhr as four: He said: He prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"4232","From al-Thawri, he said: When a traveler leading residents prays one rakah with them, then his ablution breaks and he puts forward a man who missed a rakah, though he should not have put forward except one who caught it, but he put this one forward, then he prays with them the rest of his prayer, then steps back and puts forward a man from those who caught the whole prayer, who gives salam, then he stands and makes up what he missed."
"4233","From al-Thawri, he said: When a traveler prays one rakah with residents, with a traveler and residents behind him, and he puts forward a traveler, then it occurs to the traveler to take up residence, then let him pray with them the rest of the traveler prayer, then step back and put forward a man from the travelers, who gives salam with them, then he and the residents stand and complete the rest of their prayer without an imam."
"4234","From al-Thawri concerning a Makkan man intending Kufah who travels until he reaches Yabrin al-Murtafi or thereabouts, then a need occurs to him and he returns: He said: He completes the prayer, because he did not reach a journey in which the prayer is shortened."
"4235","From al-Thawri, he said: When you are on a journey and pray one rakah by yourself, then it occurs to you to stay in that land, then complete your prayer; but if it occurs to you to leave after you intended residence, then you must complete until you leave that city."
